200c9829 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing these files. They appear to be part of a utility for resetting USB serial
200c9829 [deepseek-v4] #devices (specifically Raspberry Pi Pico based on VID `2E8`) by sending a `\nBOOT\n` command to all
200c9829 [deepseek-v4] #matching COM ports.
200c982a [deepseek-v4] #
200c982a [deepseek-v4] #Observations
200c982a [deepseek-v4] #
200c982a [deepseek-v4] #- resetcoms.cpp is self-contained and straightforward. It uses `enumerateComPorts()` from
200c982b [deepseek-v4] #`tools.h` to list serial ports, filters by device path (`"USB\\VID_2E8"`), opens each matching
200c982b [deepseek-v4] #port, and sends the boot command. The `closeComPort(h)` call is commented out – you may want to
200c982b [deepseek-v4] #restore it to avoid leaking handles (or use RAII).
200c982b [deepseek-v4] #
200c982c [deepseek-v4] #- tools.h provides a clean, platform‑agnostic abstraction for COM port operations
200c982c [deepseek-v4] #(enumerate, open, write, close). The use of `comHandle = void*` is simple but sacrifices type
200c982c [deepseek-v4] #safety. On a larger project a wrapper class would be preferable.
200c982c [deepseek-v4] #
200c982d [deepseek-v4] #- test.bat is a build script for Windows (using Ninja). Note the commented `reset.exe` and
200c982d [deepseek-v4] #the `echo.exe` call – this looks like a leftover or placeholder. The script will likely fail if
200c982d [deepseek-v4] #`echo.exe` is not intended.
200c982d [deepseek-v4] #
200c982e [deepseek-v4] #- udev.h is a sizable Linux‑only header using `libudev`. It contains a rich `Devices` class
200c982e [deepseek-v4] #for enumerating USB, tty, and input devices. This suggests the project was or will be ported to
200c982e [deepseek-v4] #Linux, but the current `resetcoms.cpp` relies on the Windows‑style `enumerateComPorts()` from
200c982e [deepseek-v4] #`tools.h`, which is not defined in the provided files.
200c982f [deepseek-v4] #
200c982f [deepseek-v4] #Potential concerns
200c982f [deepseek-v4] #
200c9830 [deepseek-v4] #1. Missing implementation – `enumerateComPorts()` and
200c9830 [deepseek-v4] #`openComPort()`/`writeComPort()`/`closeComPort()` are declared in `tools.h` but not defined in the
200c9830 [deepseek-v4] #shared snippet. This would prevent the project from compiling.
200c9830 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9831 [deepseek-v4] #2. Cross‑platform mix – Including `udev.h` alongside a Windows‑style abstraction indicates
200c9831 [deepseek-v4] #a dual‑platform intent, but the compilation script and the `tools.h` signatures are
200c9831 [deepseek-v4] #Windows‑oriented. The `udev.h` header is large and pulls in many Linux‑specific headers; if used on
200c9831 [deepseek-v4] #Windows it will not compile.
200c9832 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9832 [deepseek-v4] #3. Style hints – The code uses tabs, named functions, and avoids `std::map` with lambdas –
200c9832 [deepseek-v4] #consistent with your stated preferences. The `toBytes` inline function is fine, though you could
200c9832 [deepseek-v4] #write the constructor call directly.
200c9833 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9833 [deepseek-v4] #4. Resource management – `closeComPort` is commented out; even if the process exits
200c9833 [deepseek-v4] #quickly, it’s good practice to release handles. Consider using a `unique_ptr` with a custom deleter
200c9833 [deepseek-v4] #or at least restoring the close call.
200c9834 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9834 [deepseek-v4] #Is the content new or different?
200c9834 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9834 [deepseek-v4] #The overall pattern – iterating over serial ports, filtering by hardware ID, sending a specific
200c9835 [deepseek-v4] #string – is a standard approach for device‑specific USB‑serial reset. It is not novel but is
200c9835 [deepseek-v4] #correct. The code is concise and readable.
200c9835 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9835 [deepseek-v4] #The `udev.h` file, on the other hand, is quite extensive and includes many features (channel
