200c9ecb [deepseek-v4] #
200c9ecb [deepseek-v4] #A few observations and friendly suggestions:
200c9ecc [deepseek-v4] #
200c9ecc [deepseek-v4] #1. Program name mismatch – The shared source file is `resetcoms.cpp` and prints `resetcoms
200c9ecc [deepseek-v4] #0.1` as its header. The output shows `echocoms 0.1`. Either you compiled a different source, or
200c9ecc [deepseek-v4] #there is another executable present. For consistency, you may want to check which source
200c9ecd [deepseek-v4] #corresponds to the binary in `bin/`.
200c9ecd [deepseek-v4] #
200c9ecd [deepseek-v4] #2. Vendor ID matching – The program correctly ignored COM1 (path `ACPI\PNP0501\0`) because
200c9ecd [deepseek-v4] #it doesn’t start with `USB\VID_2E8`. COM5 was accepted (likely because its device path begins with
200c9ece [deepseek-v4] #`USB\VID_2E8`). If COM5 is indeed a Raspberry Pi Pico, the command `\nBOOT\n` will trigger a reboot
200c9ece [deepseek-v4] #into bootloader mode. That is the intended behaviour.
200c9ece [deepseek-v4] #
200c9ece [deepseek-v4] #3. Handle leak – The code in `resetcoms.cpp` intentionally leaves `closeComPort(h);`
200c9ecf [deepseek-v4] #commented. For a short‑lived program that exits immediately, the OS will clean up the handle
200c9ecf [deepseek-v4] #automatically. However, if you ever extend the tool to watch multiple ports or run continuously,
200c9ecf [deepseek-v4] #you should restore the close call or use RAII (e.g., `std::unique_ptr` with a custom deleter).
200c9ecf [deepseek-v4] #Since the output shows handle `0x118`, the handle was opened and the program then exits; on Windows
200c9ed0 [deepseek-v4] #the handle will be reclaimed. No immediate problem, but good practice to close it.
200c9ed0 [deepseek-v4] #
200c9ed0 [deepseek-v4] #4. Output formatting – The output shows `echo.exeechocoms` with no space. This often
200c9ed0 [deepseek-v4] #happens when the program prints without a newline at the very start, or when the command prompt
200c9ed1 [deepseek-v4] #concatenates its own prompt (`echo.exe`) with the program’s first output. You may want to ensure
200c9ed1 [deepseek-v4] #`std::cout` flushes quickly, or just add a `std::endl` after the first line. Already done in the
