200db315 [deepseek-v4] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.024 473.4KB 23.11s
200db315 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing the full project structure. Here are my observations:
200db316 [deepseek-v4] #
200db316 [deepseek-v4] #Overall structure
200db316 [deepseek-v4] #The project is a well‑organised RP2350 video subsystem with a separate `tools` folder for host‑side
200db316 [deepseek-v4] #utilities. The core video pipeline uses several PIO state machines in series (vidbit → pixelpump →
200db317 [deepseek-v4] #onebit/threepin/chroma). This matches the expected dataflow for generating PAL 576i video.
200db317 [deepseek-v4] #
200db317 [deepseek-v4] #New or notable content
200db317 [deepseek-v4] #- PIO programs are the most interesting part. Many use RP2350‑v1 features: `.fifo txget` /
200db318 [deepseek-v4] #`.fifo txput` (indexed RX FIFO access), cross‑PIO IRQ (`irq next set`, `irq prev clear`), and `mov
200db318 [deepseek-v4] #pindirs, …`. This confirms you are targeting the RP2350’s extended PIO capabilities.
200db318 [deepseek-v4] #- `pal576i.cpp` and `pal576i.h` contain a full interlaced frame buffer (even/odd
200db318 [deepseek-v4] #fields) with 2‑bits‑per‑pixel rendering. The dither table (5 levels) and the `ditherMem` array show
200db319 [deepseek-v4] #a deliberate greyscale approach.
200db319 [deepseek-v4] #- `vidbit.cpp` includes the system font (`sysfontMem`) as a raw 256×16‑byte bitmap, plus a
200db319 [deepseek-v4] #set of helper functions (`drawFramei2`, `circlei2`, `mtexti2`). The `mtexti2` function uses a
200db319 [deepseek-v4] #vector‑based small font (`incitti`) from `shapes.h` – this is new to me.
200db31a [deepseek-v4] #- `relay.log` documents a long development history including debugging of Windows COM port
200db31a [deepseek-v4] #enumeration and serial comms. This is not code but provides context.
200db31a [deepseek-v4] #- `rp2350.txt` and `rp2350.md` are reference material (PIO spec) included in the
200db31a [deepseek-v4] #repo, likely for offline use.
200db31b [deepseek-v4] #
200db31b [deepseek-v4] #Observations
200db31b [deepseek-v4] #- Many PIO programs are still minimal or placeholder (e.g., `spdif.pio` contains only `nop [1]`).
200db31b [deepseek-v4] #This suggests the project is actively evolving.
200db31c [deepseek-v4] #- The `interleave.pio` and `hilo32.pio` files are cryptic – their purpose in the video pipeline is
200db31c [deepseek-v4] #not immediately clear. Possibly they handle data format conversion or pulse‑width measurement.
200db31c [deepseek-v4] #- The host‑side tools (`echocoms`, `reset`, `echo`) are Windows‑specific but the presence of
200db31c [deepseek-v4] #`connections.h` and `udev.h` indicates planned Linux support (perhaps already tested).
200db31d [deepseek-v4] #- The JSON parser (`json.h` / `nitro.h`) appears to be used internally; it handles
200db31d [deepseek-v4] #`uint64_t`/`double` differentiation.
200db31d [deepseek-v4] #
200db31d [deepseek-v4] #Questions
200db31e [deepseek-v4] #- Is the `interleave` PIO intended to merge two 32‑bit streams into a single 64‑bit word? The
200db31e [deepseek-v4] #current code seems incomplete (`out y,4` etc.).
200db31e [deepseek-v4] #- The `vidbit.cpp` system font array is large (inlined). Would you prefer it as a separate binary
200db31e [deepseek-v4] #blob loaded at runtime?
200db31f [deepseek-v4] #
200db31f [deepseek-v4] #If you want me to analyse any particular PIO program in depth, or help with the host‑side serial
200db31f [deepseek-v4] #protocol, just let me know.
200db320 [deepseek-v4] #
