200e56b9 [deepseek-v4] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.040 732.6KB 18.86s
200e56b9 [deepseek-v4] #Thank you for sharing this. The dsptool/native project is significantly different from the vidbit
200e56b9 [deepseek-v4] #project in both scope and platform.
200e56ba [deepseek-v4] #
200e56ba [deepseek-v4] #• What’s new/different vs. vidbit:*
200e56ba [deepseek-v4] #
200e56ba [deepseek-v4] #- Linux‑native, not embedded. It runs on desktop Linux (x86_64, aarch64) with direct ALSA
200e56bb [deepseek-v4] #audio, DRM/EGL/OpenGL ES graphics, V4L2 video capture, and udev device enumeration. No PIO, no
200e56bb [deepseek-v4] #RP2350.
200e56bb [deepseek-v4] #- Full HTTP + JSON‑RPC server (`headless.h`, `http.h`). The core is a background daemon
200e56bb [deepseek-v4] #`fit3` that serves a web application and provides an RPC API over HTTP. This is a very different
200e56bc [deepseek-v4] #architecture from vidbit’s low‑level microcontroller firmware.
200e56bc [deepseek-v4] #- Audio‑focused. Deep ALSA integration, WAV/FLAC generation, synth, sweeping sine waves,
200e56bc [deepseek-v4] #and test PCM in/out. This project seems designed for audio DSP testing and streaming.
200e56bc [deepseek-v4] #- Rich external dependencies. libhiredis, OpenSSL, libudev, libbluetooth, libxml2, libFLAC,
200e56bd [deepseek-v4] #libcec, libgbm, libEGL, GLES2, pcap – all linked statically or dynamically. Much larger than the
200e56bd [deepseek-v4] #vidbit toolchain.
200e56bd [deepseek-v4] #- Cross‑platform (partially). Compilation paths for macOS (Apple Silicon) and Windows
200e56bd [deepseek-v4] #(Win32 COM ports, Winsock) are present alongside the primary Linux build.
200e56be [deepseek-v4] #- Heavy use of C++17. The code uses `std::optional`, `std::variant`‑like patterns, and RAII
200e56be [deepseek-v4] #in many places. It is more modern than the vidbit pure‑C and assembly‑heavy style.
200e56be [deepseek-v4] #- File system scanning, caching, blob storage via Redis (Valkey). The server stores content
200e56be [deepseek-v4] #and configuration in a key‑value store, whereas vidbit is bare‑metal with no external storage.
200e56bf [deepseek-v4] #
200e56bf [deepseek-v4] #• Observations on the provided code:*
200e56bf [deepseek-v4] #
200e56bf [deepseek-v4] #- The HTTP/RPC server is quite complete and could be extracted as a standalone library.
200e56c0 [deepseek-v4] #- Several files (`opensocket.cpp`, `headless.h`) mix platform‑specific code with `#ifdef` blocks,
200e56c0 [deepseek-v4] #which works but can become maintenance‑heavy.
200e56c0 [deepseek-v4] #- The `Scribe`/`Sink` classes for Redis pub/sub are not thread‑safe in their current form (shared
200e56c0 [deepseek-v4] #non‑atomic `from` variable in `Sink::run`).
200e56c1 [deepseek-v4] #- The `Process` class (in `headless.h`) spawns external binaries via `vfork`/`execvp` – elegant but
200e56c1 [deepseek-v4] #has some commented‑out thread management that looks incomplete.
200e56c1 [deepseek-v4] #- `wav.h` and `synth.h` are well separated and could be reused in other projects.
200e56c1 [deepseek-v4] #
200e56c2 [deepseek-v4] #• Potential questions:*
200e56c2 [deepseek-v4] #- Is the HTTP server intended to be the primary interface, or are there plans for other transports
200e56c2 [deepseek-v4] #(WebSocket)?
200e56c2 [deepseek-v4] #- The `stereo.h` file is missing (only `headless.h` included). Is the `Stereo` class meant to be a
200e56c3 [deepseek-v4] #singleton, or instantiated per‑connection?
200e56c3 [deepseek-v4] #- Many of the PCIe/DRM/GBM functions are only stubbed or error‑checked; is the display pipeline
200e56c3 [deepseek-v4] #(DRM + EGL) working end‑to‑end currently?
200e56c3 [deepseek-v4] #
200e56c4 [deepseek-v4] #Overall, this is a substantial, cross‑platform DSP server with a web UI, quite distinct from the
200e56c4 [deepseek-v4] #vidbit microcontroller work. If you’d like a deeper review of any specific subsystem (e.g., audio
200e56c4 [deepseek-v4] #streaming, HTTP routing, DRM rendering), I can go into more detail.
200e56c4 [deepseek-v4] #
