201239e0 [deepseek-v4] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 585B 9.72s
201239e0 [deepseek-v4] #The error usually occurs because `utf8` is an incomplete type at the point where
201239e1 [deepseek-v4] #`std::vector<utf8>` is instantiated. In C++, `std::vector<T>` requires `T` to be a complete
201239e1 [deepseek-v4] #type (its full definition must be visible) because the vector stores objects by value—it needs
201239e1 [deepseek-v4] #to know the size and layout of `T` to allocate memory, call constructors/destructors, etc.
201239e2 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e2 [deepseek-v4] #In your code:
201239e2 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e2 [deepseek-v4] #struct JSObject {
201239e3 [deepseek-v4] #    std::vector<utf8> names;   // <-- utf8 must be complete
201239e3 [deepseek-v4] #    std::vector<JSValue*> values; // <-- JSValue* is fine even if JSValue is incomplete
201239e3 [deepseek-v4] #    virtual ~JSObject() {}
201239e3 [deepseek-v4] #};
201239e4 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e4 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e4 [deepseek-v4] #- `std::vector<JSValue*>` only needs the size of a pointer, which is known even if `JSValue` is
201239e4 [deepseek-v4] #only forward‑declared.
201239e5 [deepseek-v4] #- `std::vector<utf8>` needs the full definition of `utf8`. If `utf8` is merely declared (e.g.,
201239e5 [deepseek-v4] #`class utf8;`) or not defined at all, you get an incomplete type error.
201239e5 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e5 [deepseek-v4] #• Common fixes*:
201239e6 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e6 [deepseek-v4] #1. Include the full definition of `utf8` (or `JSValue` if it appears as a value type
201239e6 [deepseek-v4] #elsewhere).
201239e6 [deepseek-v4] #   For example, if `utf8` is an alias for `std::string`, make sure you have `#include <string>` and
201239e7 [deepseek-v4] #`using utf8 = std::string;` before the struct definition.
201239e7 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e7 [deepseek-v4] #2. Forward‑declare correctly if `utf8` is a class defined in another header. You cannot use
201239e7 [deepseek-v4] #it in a `std::vector` with only a forward declaration.
201239e8 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e8 [deepseek-v4] #3. Check for missing includes – if `utf8` is defined in your own project, ensure its header
201239e8 [deepseek-v4] #is included before `JSObject`.
201239e8 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e9 [deepseek-v4] #If you intended `utf8` to be a primitive type (like `char8_t*`), then the vector should be
201239e9 [deepseek-v4] #`std::vector<char8_t>` or similar, but the name `utf8` suggests a custom string type.
201239e9 [deepseek-v4] #
201239e9 [deepseek-v4] #Please share the definition (or declaration) of `utf8` and any relevant includes, and I can give
201239ea [deepseek-v4] #more specific advice.
201239ea [deepseek-v4] #
20123acd [nitro@ryzen5] typedef std::string utf8;
20123b30 [deepseek-v4] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 2.561KB 5.66s
20123b30 [deepseek-v4] #The fact that `utf8` is a `typedef` for `std::string` is key. The "incomplete type" error you saw
20123b31 [deepseek-v4] #almost certainly means that the full definition of `std::string` (or the `typedef` itself) was
20123b31 [deepseek-v4] #not visible at the point where `std::vector<utf8>` was instantiated.
20123b31 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b32 [deepseek-v4] #In C++, `std::vector<T>` requires `T` to be a complete type—the compiler must know its
20123b32 [deepseek-v4] #size, default constructor, destructor, etc. Since `std::string` needs its own includes
20123b32 [deepseek-v4] #(`<string>`), the most common cause is:
20123b32 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b33 [deepseek-v4] #1. Missing `#include <string>` before the struct definition.
20123b33 [deepseek-v4] #2. The `typedef std::string utf8;` appears after the struct, or inside a different
20123b33 [deepseek-v4] #namespace / scope than where the vector is declared.
20123b33 [deepseek-v4] #3. The typedef might be conditional or hidden behind a preprocessor guard.
20123b34 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b34 [deepseek-v4] #• To fix it, place the following before* `struct JSObject`:
20123b34 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b34 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b35 [deepseek-v4] ##include <string>
20123b35 [deepseek-v4] #typedef std::string utf8;
20123b35 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b35 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b36 [deepseek-v4] #Then your struct compiles correctly because `std::string` is now a complete type.
20123b36 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b36 [deepseek-v4] #If you already have both, double‑check that there’s no circular include, and that the `typedef` is
20123b36 [deepseek-v4] #not inside a forward‑declared class or forgotten namespace. The error message should point exactly
20123b37 [deepseek-v4] #to the line where `std::vector<utf8>` is declared—that line is where the compiler noticed the
20123b37 [deepseek-v4] #incomplete type.
20123b37 [deepseek-v4] #
20123b37 [deepseek-v4] #If you still have issues, please share the exact compiler error text and the order of
20123b38 [deepseek-v4] #includes/definitions in your file. I’m happy to help further.
20123b38 [deepseek-v4] #
20124391 [nitro@ryzen5] is there std mutex in pico rp2350?
20124444 [deepseek-v4] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  deepseek-v4 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 4.229KB 10.75s
20124445 [deepseek-v4] #No, `std::mutex` is not available by default on the Raspberry Pi Pico RP2350 when using the
20124445 [deepseek-v4] #standard Pico SDK and toolchain (e.g., `arm-none-eabi-g++` with Newlib‑nano or picolibc).
20124445 [deepseek-v4] #
20124445 [deepseek-v4] #Why?
20124446 [deepseek-v4] #
20124446 [deepseek-v4] #- `std::mutex` depends on threading primitives provided by an operating system (e.g.,
20124446 [deepseek-v4] #`pthread_mutex` on Linux) or a C++ runtime that supports `std::thread`.
20124446 [deepseek-v4] #- The bare‑metal Pico SDK does not include a threading implementation — it runs directly on
20124447 [deepseek-v4] #hardware.
20124447 [deepseek-v4] #- The C++ standard library (newlib‑nano / picolibc) shipped with the GNU ARM embedded toolchain
20124447 [deepseek-v4] #does not implement `std::mutex` correctly in this environment. The compiler will either
20124447 [deepseek-v4] #reject it (compile‑time error) or emit a linker error for missing symbols.
20124448 [deepseek-v4] #
20124448 [deepseek-v4] #What you can use instead
20124448 [deepseek-v4] #
20124448 [deepseek-v4] #The Pico SDK provides efficient, hardware‑supported synchronization primitives for the RP2350’s
20124449 [deepseek-v4] #dual‑core architecture:
20124449 [deepseek-v4] #
20124449 [deepseek-v4] #╭─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2012444a [deepseek-v4] #│  Primitive              │  Description                                       │
2012444a [deepseek-v4] #├─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2012444a [deepseek-v4] #│  `mutex_t`              │  Blocking mutex (uses SDK scheduler if available)  │
2012444a [deepseek-v4] #│  `critical_section_t`   │  Disables interrupts (spinlock)                    │
2012444b [deepseek-v4] #│  `spin_lock_t`          │  Hardware spinlock (for low‑level synchronization) │
2012444b [deepseek-v4] #│  `sync.h` functions     │  `__sync_*` builtins, atomic operations            │
2012444b [deepseek-v4] #╰─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2012444b [deepseek-v4] #
2012444c [deepseek-v4] #Example using `mutex_t`:
2012444c [deepseek-v4] #
2012444c [deepseek-v4] ##include "pico/mutex.h"
2012444c [deepseek-v4] #
2012444d [deepseek-v4] #mutex_t my_mutex;
2012444d [deepseek-v4] #mutex_init(&my_mutex);
2012444d [deepseek-v4] #
2012444d [deepseek-v4] #mutex_enter_blocking(&my_mutex);
2012444e [deepseek-v4] #// critical section
2012444e [deepseek-v4] #mutex_exit(&my_mutex);
2012444e [deepseek-v4] #
2012444e [deepseek-v4] #
2012444f [deepseek-v4] #What about FreeRTOS / other RTOS?
2012444f [deepseek-v4] #
2012444f [deepseek-v4] #If you add an RTOS like FreeRTOS (e.g., using `pico-enable-freertos` or manual integration),
2012444f [deepseek-v4] #`std::mutex` may become available — but that requires:
20124450 [deepseek-v4] #
20124450 [deepseek-v4] #- The RTOS must provide a `pthread_mutex` or equivalent adaptation layer.
20124450 [deepseek-v4] #- The C++ library must be configured with `_POSIX_THREADS` support (often not the default with
20124450 [deepseek-v4] #newlib‑nano).
20124451 [deepseek-v4] #- In practice, most Pico + FreeRTOS projects still use the SDK's `mutex_t` or FreeRTOS’s own
20124451 [deepseek-v4] #`xSemaphoreCreateMutex` rather than `std::mutex`, because the C++ library support is incomplete.
20124451 [deepseek-v4] #
20124451 [deepseek-v4] #Summary
20124452 [deepseek-v4] #
20124452 [deepseek-v4] #- Bare‑metal: No `std::mutex`. Use SDK primitives (`mutex_t`, `critical_section`,
20124452 [deepseek-v4] #`spin_lock`).
20124452 [deepseek-v4] #- With FreeRTOS: Possibly, but not guaranteed; RTOS‑specific mutexes are safer and simpler.
20124453 [deepseek-v4] #
20124453 [deepseek-v4] #If you need C++ standard threading, consider a different platform (e.g., Linux, ESP32 with ESP‑IDF)
20124453 [deepseek-v4] #where `std::mutex` works out of the box.
20124453 [deepseek-v4] #
