20b3575b [nitro@ryzen5] adding vga output to microcontroller can i start with just i2c 3 wire?
20b358ba [kimi-k2.7-code] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.005 2.974KB 21.24s
20b358ba [kimi-k2.7-code] #You can start simple, but I need to clarify: I²C is not used for VGA video output. The I²C
20b358ba [kimi-k2.7-code] #pins on a VGA connector (pins 9, 12, 15) are only for DDC/EDID — they let the monitor tell
20b358ba [kimi-k2.7-code] #the source its capabilities. The actual video uses analog RGB + sync.
20b358bb [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358bb [kimi-k2.7-code] #So if you want VGA video output from a microcontroller, the absolute minimum is:
20b358bb [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358bb [kimi-k2.7-code] #Minimum working VGA: 3 signal wires + ground
20b358bc [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358bc [kimi-k2.7-code] #You can start with just one color, plus the two sync signals:
20b358bc [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358bc [kimi-k2.7-code] #╭───────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────╮
20b358bd [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Signal               │  Pin         │  MCU connection               │
20b358bd [kimi-k2.7-code] #├───────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
20b358bd [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Green (or Red/Blue)  │  2 (or 1/3)  │  DAC / PWM / resistor ladder  │
20b358bd [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  H-sync               │  13          │  GPIO timer output            │
20b358be [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  V-sync               │  14          │  GPIO timer output            │
20b358be [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Ground               │  5, 6–8, 10  │  MCU GND                      │
20b358be [kimi-k2.7-code] #╰───────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────╯
20b358be [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358bf [kimi-k2.7-code] #This gives you monochrome output. Green is usually best because many monitors use it for
20b358bf [kimi-k2.7-code] #sync-on-green detection and it tends to look cleanest.
20b358bf [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358bf [kimi-k2.7-code] #If you want color
20b358c0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #You need three analog outputs for R, G, B, plus H-sync and V-sync:
20b358c0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #╭──────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────╮
20b358c1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Signal  │  Pin         │  Notes          │
20b358c1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #├──────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────┤
20b358c1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Red     │  1           │  0–0.7 V, 75 Ω  │
20b358c1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Green   │  2           │  0–0.7 V, 75 Ω  │
20b358c2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Blue    │  3           │  0–0.7 V, 75 Ω  │
20b358c2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  H-sync  │  13          │  5 V TTL        │
20b358c2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  V-sync  │  14          │  5 V TTL        │
20b358c2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  GND     │  5, 6–8, 10  │                 │
20b358c3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #╰──────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────╯
20b358c3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #What you actually need from the MCU
20b358c3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- Two sync pins that can generate precise timing (often a timer with PWM or direct GPIO
20b358c4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #bit-banging).
20b358c4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- One or three DAC channels for the video signal. You can build these with resistor ladders
20b358c4 [kimi-k2.7-code] #(R-2R) if your MCU lacks a DAC.
20b358c5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #So to answer your question
20b358c5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- If you mean 3 wires total (one color + H-sync + V-sync): yes, that works for
20b358c6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #monochrome VGA.
20b358c6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- If you mean I²C only: no, a VGA monitor will not display video from I²C.
20b358c6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b358c6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #What microcontroller are you using? That determines whether you can use a hardware DAC, SPI
20b358c7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35d96 [nitro@ryzen5] i said to start, or is there a resoloution that all VGA monitors will support?
20b35ee5 [kimi-k2.7-code] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.006 5.634KB 19.78s
20b35ee5 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Yes — 640 × 480 at 60 Hz is the baseline resolution that every VGA monitor must support. If
20b35ee6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #you output those timings, basically any VGA display will sync to it.
20b35ee6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ee6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Minimum wiring to start
20b35ee6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ee6 [kimi-k2.7-code] #You can absolutely start with just 3 signal wires plus ground. No I²C/DDC is required.
20b35ee7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Leave pins 9, 12, and 15 unconnected — the monitor will just default to 640 × 480.
20b35ee7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ee7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #╭───────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────╮
20b35ee7 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Wire                 │  VGA pin     │  Purpose                          │
20b35ee8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #├───────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
20b35ee8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  H-sync               │  13          │  Tells monitor end of each line   │
20b35ee8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  V-sync               │  14          │  Tells monitor end of each frame  │
20b35ee8 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Green (or Red/Blue)  │  2           │  Video intensity                  │
20b35ee9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Ground               │  5, 6–8, 10  │  Common return                    │
20b35ee9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #╰───────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────╯
20b35ee9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ee9 [kimi-k2.7-code] #That gives you monochrome output. Tie the unused R and B lines to ground through 75 Ω resistors, or
20b35eea [kimi-k2.7-code] #just leave them grounded.
20b35eea [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35eea [kimi-k2.7-code] #640 × 480 @ 60 Hz timings
20b35eea [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35eeb [kimi-k2.7-code] #This is the safest “universal VGA” mode.
20b35eeb [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35eeb [kimi-k2.7-code] #╭──────────────────────────┬──────────────────╮
20b35eeb [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Parameter               │  Value           │
20b35eec [kimi-k2.7-code] #├──────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
20b35eec [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Pixel clock             │  25.175 MHz      │
20b35eec [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Horizontal active       │  640 pixels      │
20b35eec [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Horizontal front porch  │  16 pixels       │
20b35eed [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Horizontal sync         │  96 pixels       │
20b35eed [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Horizontal back porch   │  48 pixels       │
20b35eed [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  **Horizontal total**    │  **800 pixels**  │
20b35eed [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Vertical active         │  480 lines       │
20b35eee [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Vertical front porch    │  10 lines        │
20b35eee [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Vertical sync           │  2 lines         │
20b35eee [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Vertical back porch     │  33 lines        │
20b35eee [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  **Vertical total**      │  **525 lines**   │
20b35eef [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  Refresh rate            │  ~60 Hz          │
20b35eef [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  H-sync polarity         │  Negative        │
20b35eef [kimi-k2.7-code] #│  V-sync polarity         │  Negative        │
20b35eef [kimi-k2.7-code] #╰──────────────────────────┴──────────────────╯
20b35ef0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ef0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #What the monitor needs to see
20b35ef0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ef0 [kimi-k2.7-code] #During active video, the color line sits between 0 V (black) and about 0.7 V (full brightness) into
20b35ef1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #75 Ω. During blanking (porches and sync), keep it at 0 V.
20b35ef1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ef1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #The sync pulses are 5 V TTL, active low:
20b35ef1 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ef2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- H-sync: one 3.8 µs pulse at the end of every 31.8 µs line.
20b35ef2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- V-sync: two 64 µs pulses at the end of every 16.7 ms frame.
20b35ef2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ef2 [kimi-k2.7-code] #Microcontroller reality check
20b35ef3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
20b35ef3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #- 25.175 MHz pixel
20b35ef3 [kimi-k2.7-code] #
