2136c430 [grok-build-0.1] #
2136c430 [grok-build-0.1] #• 1. Star feature context*
2136c430 [grok-build-0.1] #Since you said the star feature is working, I looked at how it interacts with the new pieces:
2136c430 [grok-build-0.1] #- Stars are stored per-share in `roha.sharedFiles`.
2136c430 [grok-build-0.1] #- `commitShares` respects `starshare` + `stars.length`.
2136c431 [grok-build-0.1] #- When `/project` switches contexts, the keyed shares come back with their star data.
2136c431 [grok-build-0.1] #
2136c431 [grok-build-0.1] #One small thing I noticed: stars are still just a raw string (`"*"` etc.). That’s fine for
2136c431 [grok-build-0.1] #now, but if you ever want to do “only shares with 3+ stars” or per-user starring, you’ll probably
2136c431 [grok-build-0.1] #want to normalize it.
2136c432 [grok-build-0.1] #
2136c432 [grok-build-0.1] #• 2. slopprompt.ts*
2136c432 [grok-build-0.1] #This is carrying a lot of weight. A few things worth calling out:
2136c433 [grok-build-0.1] #
2136c433 [grok-build-0.1] #- You have three different width functions (`stringWidth`, `discordStringWidth`, and the internal
2136c433 [grok-build-0.1] #`isDoubleWidth`/`discordWide`). There’s some overlap and special-case exceptions. This is the kind
2136c433 [grok-build-0.1] #of thing that tends to drift.
2136c434 [grok-build-0.1] #- The raw mode + multi-connection receiver logic with `Promise.race` is clever but easy to get into
2136c434 [grok-build-0.1] #weird states (especially on paste or disconnect).
