2138d00a [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.8.8 ⛲  grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.004 12.52KB 18.27s
2138d00a [grok-build-0.1] #• Code review for `json.h`*
2138d00a [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d00a [grok-build-0.1] #The parser is functional for basic cases but has several structural and robustness issues. Here's a
2138d00b [grok-build-0.1] #breakdown:
2138d00b [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d00b [grok-build-0.1] #Issues
2138d00b [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d00b [grok-build-0.1] #1. String value handling is fragile
2138d00c [grok-build-0.1] #   - String parsing always writes into `name`.
2138d00c [grok-build-0.1] #   - Value strings only work because the `,` / `]` / `}` handling falls back to `name` when
2138d00c [grok-build-0.1] #`literal` is empty.
2138d00d [grok-build-0.1] #   - This is implicit and easy to break (e.g. with whitespace, empty strings, or certain error
2138d00d [grok-build-0.1] #paths).
2138d00d [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d00d [grok-build-0.1] #2. Unused / incomplete code
2138d00e [grok-build-0.1] #   - `parseString()` is defined but never called.
2138d00e [grok-build-0.1] #   - The main string parser inside `parseJSON` has its own (partial) unescaping logic.
2138d00e [grok-build-0.1] #   - `\uXXXX` handling is just a TODO + `cout`.
2138d00e [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d00f [grok-build-0.1] #3. Error handling
2138d00f [grok-build-0.1] #   - Errors are reported via `std::cout` and early returns.
2138d00f [grok-build-0.1] #   - No structured error information is returned to the caller (just `int` error codes with no
2138d00f [grok-build-0.1] #meaning documented).
2138d010 [grok-build-0.1] #   - The function can leak memory on error paths because objects/arrays on the stacks are not
2138d010 [grok-build-0.1] #cleaned up.
2138d010 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d011 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Memory management
2138d011 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Raw `new`/`delete` with manual ownership transfer.
2138d011 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `JSValue` destructor deletes its object/array, but the stacks in `parseJSON` can leave objects
2138d011 [grok-build-0.1] #behind on parse failure.
2138d012 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d012 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Deprecated / problematic APIs
2138d012 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `std::wstring_convert` + `codecvt_utf8` is deprecated since C++17 and removed in some
2138d012 [grok-build-0.1] #implementations.
2138d013 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Global `convert32` instance.
2138d013 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d013 [grok-build-0.1] #6. Other observations
2138d013 [grok-build-0.1] #   - No support for `NaN`, `Infinity`, or leading `+` in numbers (follows the "integer vs number"
2138d014 [grok-build-0.1] #distinction you mentioned).
2138d014 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Control character check (`c < 9`) is too aggressive (tabs and newlines are valid whitespace).
2138d014 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `toJSON()` does not escape strings properly in all cases (it calls `escape(...)` which isn't
2138d015 [grok-build-0.1] #defined in this file).
2138d015 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `stringMember()` falls back to `toJSON()` for non-string members, which is surprising
2138d015 [grok-build-0.1] #behavior.
2138d015 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d016 [grok-build-0.1] #Suggested direction
2138d016 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d016 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to:
2138d016 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d016 [grok-build-0.1] #- A. Propose a cleaner parser structure (still using the same
2138d017 [grok-build-0.1] #`JSValue`/`JSObject`/`JSArray` types)?
2138d017 [grok-build-0.1] #- B. Focus on fixing the current parser with minimal changes?
2138d017 [grok-build-0.1] #- C. Improve specific parts (string unescaping, error reporting, memory safety, etc.)?
2138d017 [grok-build-0.1] #- D. Something else (e.g. adding `fromJSON` improvements, better number handling, etc.)?
2138d018 [grok-build-0.1] #
2138d018 [grok-build-0.1] #Just tell me which direction you want and how aggressive the changes should be. I won't dump a full
2138d018 [grok-build-0.1] #rewrite unless requested.
2138d019 [grok-build-0.1] #
