2140caef [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ nitrologic Relay 1.9.0 ⛲  grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.248 815.6KB 23.28s
2140caef [grok-build-0.1] #• Thanks for the expanded drop.* This is a much deeper look at the full `vidbit` system.
2140caef [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf0 [grok-build-0.1] #High-level picture (what's new)
2140caf0 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf0 [grok-build-0.1] #You're building a PIO-heavy PAL 576i/288p generator on the RP2350 with a multi-stage video
2140caf0 [grok-build-0.1] #pipeline:
2140caf1 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `vidbit.pio` → master sync + active-line IRQ
2140caf1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `pixelpump.pio` → feeds two 32-bit "wave trains" (color 0/1 bit patterns)
2140caf1 [grok-build-0.1] #- `onebit.pio` → per-pixel mux between the two trains (using a loopback pin)
2140caf2 [grok-build-0.1] #- `threepin.pio` → drives 3 tristate pins (resistor DAC for Y/Pb/Pr or composite + chroma)
2140caf2 [grok-build-0.1] #- `chroma.pio` / `colortrain.pio` / `pinpump.pio` → color burst + PDM-style chroma
2140caf2 [grok-build-0.1] #- `wst.pio` → teletext insertion
2140caf2 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf3 [grok-build-0.1] #Plus supporting paths for:
2140caf3 [grok-build-0.1] #- Audio (I2S + simple speaker PIO)
2140caf3 [grok-build-0.1] #- PS/2 input
2140caf3 [grok-build-0.1] #- 1-bit vs 2-bit rendering paths (`monobit.` vs `pal576i.`)
2140caf4 [grok-build-0.1] #- JSON-RPC control surface
2140caf4 [grok-build-0.1] #- Host tooling (COM port enumeration, reset, echo, RPC client)
2140caf4 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf4 [grok-build-0.1] #The resistor-DAC approach (using `pindirs` tricks to turn pins on/off into a crude DAC) is quite
2140caf5 [grok-build-0.1] #aggressive.
2140caf5 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf5 [grok-build-0.1] #Notable observations
2140caf5 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf6 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Very sophisticated PIO chaining
2140caf6 [grok-build-0.1] #   The `pixelpump → onebit → threepin` pipeline is clever but fragile. Any change to line count,
2140caf6 [grok-build-0.1] #pixel format, or timing constants can desynchronise the whole chain.
2140caf6 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf7 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Duplication is getting painful
2140caf7 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `monobit.cpp` / `pal576i.cpp` have near-duplicate `plot`/`rect`/`text`/`drawC64Char` families.
2140caf7 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `flipBits` vs `flipBits2` + `initFlipBits2`.
2140caf7 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Two completely separate framebuffers and drawing paths (`evenFrame`/`oddFrame` vs
2140caf8 [grok-build-0.1] #progressive).
2140caf8 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf8 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Timing constants are scattered
2140caf8 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `vidbit.pio` has `HSYNC_WAIT29`, `BACKPORCH_WAIT32`, etc.
2140caf9 [grok-build-0.1] #   - `pal576i.cpp` has its own preamble counts, sync pulse values, and line counts.
2140caf9 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Some of these are duplicated or slightly different between 288p and 576i.
2140caf9 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caf9 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Host tooling is actually useful
2140cafa [grok-build-0.1] #   The `tools/` stuff (especially `enumports.cpp` + `resetcoms.cpp` / `echocoms.cpp`) is practical.
2140cafa [grok-build-0.1] #Detecting VID `2E8A` and blasting `\nBOOT\n` over all matching COM ports is a nice dev workflow.
2140cafa [grok-build-0.1] #
2140cafa [grok-build-0.1] #5. Some things are still stubs
2140cafb [grok-build-0.1] #   - `receiveBuffer()` in `pal576i.cpp` is empty.
2140cafb [grok-build-0.1] #   - Several `#ifdef` paths (WST, COLORTRAIN, ONEBIT, etc.) are only partially wired.
2140cafb [grok-build-0.1] #
2140cafc [grok-build-0.1] #Questions / areas worth discussing
2140cafc [grok-build-0.1] #
2140cafc [grok-build-0.1] #- Are you trying to get full 2-bit colour working via the resistor DAC, or are you mostly
2140cafc [grok-build-0.1] #validating the 1-bit path first?
2140cafd [grok-build-0.1] #- The current `pixelpump + onebit + threepin` design is very specific to 1-bit pixels with two
2140cafd [grok-build-0.1] #precomputed wave trains. Do you want to keep that model, or move toward a more general "pump N bits
2140cafd [grok-build-0.1] #per pixel" design?
2140cafd [grok-build-0.1] #- How married are you to the current split between `monobit.` and `pal576i.`? Consolidating
2140cafe [grok-build-0.1] #the drawing primitives would make maintenance much easier.
2140cafe [grok-build-0.1] #- Clock is 166.5 MHz for exact PAL subcarrier math — are you planning to support other system
2140cafe [grok-build-0.1] #clocks later, or is this locked?
2140cafe [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caff [grok-build-0.1] #What would be most useful right now?
2140caff [grok-build-0.1] #
2140caff [grok-build-0.1] #Pick one (or suggest something else):
2140caff [grok-build-0.1] #
2140cb00 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Review the video pipeline (`pixelpump` → `onebit` → `threepin` + chroma) for timing
2140cb00 [grok-build-0.1] #hazards or simplification opportunities.
2140cb00 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Help consolidate the two rendering paths (1-bit vs 2-bit) into a cleaner abstraction.
2140cb00 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Audit the PIO timing constants and see if we can derive more of them from a single
2140cb01 [grok-build-0.1] #source of truth.
2140cb01 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Look at the host tools — improve VID/PID matching, add proper close handling, or make
2140cb01 [grok-build-0.1] #the RPC side more robust.
2140cb01 [grok-build-0.1] #5. Something specific (e.g. "why is my chroma burst not locking", "how do I add proper
2140cb02 [grok-build-0.1] #interlaced field handling", "teletext insertion plan", etc.).
2140cb02 [grok-build-0.1] #
2140cb02 [grok-build-0.1] #Just tell me which direction you want to go. The project has grown into something quite
2140cb02 [grok-build-0.1] #substantial.
2140cb03 [grok-build-0.1] #
