21481a55 [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.113 349.2KB 9.27s
21481a55 [grok-build-0.1] #• Yes, that's a helpful and important constraint.* It changes the shape of the architecture
21481a55 [grok-build-0.1] #meaningfully.
21481a56 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a56 [grok-build-0.1] #What this tells us
21481a56 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a56 [grok-build-0.1] #- The relay is the long-lived owner of conversation history and project state.
21481a57 [grok-build-0.1] #- The bots are more transient — they can restart, reconnect, or be added/removed without
21481a57 [grok-build-0.1] #losing the discussion.
21481a57 [grok-build-0.1] #- Bots should be treated more like frontends / interfaces rather than stateful
21481a57 [grok-build-0.1] #participants.
21481a58 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a58 [grok-build-0.1] #This leans the design toward the relay being the source of truth, and the bots being
21481a58 [grok-build-0.1] #relatively lightweight connectors between Discord and the relay.
21481a58 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a59 [grok-build-0.1] #Implications for the current problem (unison responses)
21481a59 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a59 [grok-build-0.1] #This helps clarify a few things:
21481a59 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a5a [grok-build-0.1] #1. @mention filtering becomes cleaner
21481a5a [grok-build-0.1] #   Since history lives in the relay, the bots don't need to maintain their own view of the
21481a5a [grok-build-0.1] #conversation to decide whether to respond. They can make a simple local decision ("Was I
21481a5a [grok-build-0.1] #mentioned?") and only forward relevant messages.
21481a5b [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a5b [grok-build-0.1] #2. Bots can be simpler
21481a5b [grok-build-0.1] #   They don't need to carry much context themselves. Their main jobs are:
21481a5b [grok-build-0.1] #   - Listen for Discord events
21481a5c [grok-build-0.1] #   - Forward @mentions + commands to the relay
21481a5c [grok-build-0.1] #   - Take messages from the relay and post them to Discord
21481a5c [grok-build-0.1] #   - Identify themselves clearly when they connect
21481a5c [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a5d [grok-build-0.1] #3. Routing can live in the relay
21481a5d [grok-build-0.1] #   Because the relay has the full history and context, it becomes a natural place to make decisions
21481a5d [grok-build-0.1] #like:
21481a5d [grok-build-0.1] #   - Which bot should reply
21481a5e [grok-build-0.1] #   - Whether a message needs special handling
21481a5e [grok-build-0.1] #   - Maintaining per-bot personas or model assignments (if you go that way later)
21481a5e [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a5e [grok-build-0.1] #4. Reconnection is simpler
21481a5f [grok-build-0.1] #   A bot can reconnect and immediately be useful without having to sync history. The relay doesn't
21481a5f [grok-build-0.1] #have to treat bot restarts as significant events for the conversation.
21481a5f [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a5f [grok-build-0.1] #Questions this raises
21481a60 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a60 [grok-build-0.1] #Given that the relay owns the long-lived state, here are some useful things to decide:
21481a60 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a60 [grok-build-0.1] #- Bot identity on connect
21481a60 [grok-build-0.1] #  Should a bot announce its name (e.g. "I am sloppy") when it connects to the relay? This would let
21481a61 [grok-build-0.1] #the relay know exactly which Discord bot is on which connection.
21481a61 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a61 [grok-build-0.1] #- Message tagging
21481a61 [grok-build-0.1] #  When the relay sends something back to Discord via a bot, does it need to know which bot
21481a62 [grok-build-0.1] #to use, or is there one "main" bot for output?
21481a62 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a62 [grok-build-0.1] #- Commands vs chat
21481a63 [grok-build-0.1] #  Since history lives in the relay, should commands (like `/reset`, `/model`, `/push`) also be
21481a63 [grok-build-0.1] #processed centrally? This would mean even command messages probably need to be forwarded when they
21481a63 [grok-build-0.1] #arrive via a bot.
21481a63 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a64 [grok-build-0.1] #- Visibility of non-@ messages
21481a64 [grok-build-0.1] #  Even if bots only act on @mentions, should plain messages still be forwarded to the relay
21481a64 [grok-build-0.1] #so they can be recorded in history? Or should they be dropped at the bot level?
21481a64 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a65 [grok-build-0.1] #- Bot differentiation
21481a65 [grok-build-0.1] #  Do you see the three bots as having their own persistent identities from the relay's perspective
21481a65 [grok-build-0.1] #(e.g. each bot might eventually be tied to a specific model or persona), or are they currently just
21481a65 [grok-build-0.1] #interchangeable ways to talk to the same underlying system?
21481a66 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a66 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
21481a66 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a66 [grok-build-0.1] #This constraint (relay owns history, bots are shorter-lived) is a good foundation. It makes the
21481a67 [grok-build-0.1] #"@mention only" rule easier to implement cleanly.
21481a67 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481a67 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like to lock in a direction on any of the points above, or is there another aspect of the
21481a67 [grok-build-0.1] #longer-lived history that you think is important to consider?
21481a67 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481efe [nitro@ryzen5] it feels like obfiscation is happening, where is a bot reply being generated and emitted?
21481fd5 [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.114 352.8KB 9.81s
21481fd5 [grok-build-0.1] #• Clear answer:*
21481fd6 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fd6 [grok-build-0.1] #A bot reply is generated in one place and emitted in another.
21481fd6 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fd6 [grok-build-0.1] #Generation (where the actual text is created)
21481fd7 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fd7 [grok-build-0.1] #The model reply is generated inside `relay.ts`.
21481fd7 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fd7 [grok-build-0.1] #Specifically:
21481fd8 [grok-build-0.1] #- In the `relay(depth, from)` function.
21481fd8 [grok-build-0.1] #- After the call to the model provider (`endpoint.chat.completions.create(...)` or the equivalent
21481fd8 [grok-build-0.1] #for Anthropic/Gemini/etc.).
21481fd8 [grok-build-0.1] #- The text comes from `choice.message.content` (or the `responses` endpoint equivalent).
21481fd9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Once the content is received, it is:
21481fd9 [grok-build-0.1] #  - Added to `rohaHistory` as an assistant message.
21481fd9 [grok-build-0.1] #  - Passed to `slopBroadcast(...)`.
21481fd9 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fda [grok-build-0.1] #At this point, no Discord message has been sent yet. The relay only has the text.
21481fda [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fda [grok-build-0.1] #Emission to Discord (where it actually appears in the channel)
21481fda [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fdb [grok-build-0.1] #The text is sent to Discord inside `slopbot.ts`.
21481fdb [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fdb [grok-build-0.1] #The flow is:
21481fdb [grok-build-0.1] #1. `slopBroadcast()` in `relay.ts` writes the message over the TCP connection(s).
21481fdc [grok-build-0.1] #2. The receiving `slopbot.ts` gets it in its read loop.
21481fdc [grok-build-0.1] #3. It calls `onFountain(message)`.
21481fdc [grok-build-0.1] #4. `onFountain` calls `messageSloppy(message, userId)`.
21481fdc [grok-build-0.1] #5. `messageSloppy` does the actual `channel.send(...)` to Discord.
21481fdd [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fdd [grok-build-0.1] #Why it currently feels obfuscated
21481fdd [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fdd [grok-build-0.1] #This split is the main source of the confusion:
21481fde [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fde [grok-build-0.1] #- Generation = relay process (central, long-lived, owns history)
21481fde [grok-build-0.1] #- Emission = bot process (short-lived, one per Discord connection)
21481fde [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fdf [grok-build-0.1] #Because the relay currently does a broadcast to every connected bot, all three bots
21481fdf [grok-build-0.1] #(`sloppy`, `riff`, `foggy`) receive the same generated content and all call `messageSloppy`, which
21481fdf [grok-build-0.1] #is why they all reply.
21481fdf [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fe0 [grok-build-0.1] #There is also no strong tagging on the broadcast that says “this message is intended for this
21481fe0 [grok-build-0.1] #specific bot”.
21481fe0 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fe0 [grok-build-0.1] #Summary of the current path for a reply
21481fe1 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fe1 [grok-build-0.1] #User message in Discord
21481fe1 [grok-build-0.1] #→ `slopbot.ts` (messageCreate)
21481fe1 [grok-build-0.1] #→ TCP to relay
21481fe2 [grok-build-0.1] #→ `relay.ts` (added to history + `relay()` called)
21481fe2 [grok-build-0.1] #→ Model generates text
21481fe2 [grok-build-0.1] #→ `slopBroadcast()` (to all bots)
21481fe2 [grok-build-0.1] #→ `slopbot.ts` (onFountain → messageSloppy)
21481fe2 [grok-build-0.1] #→ Discord `channel.send()`
21481fe3 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fe3 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
21481fe3 [grok-build-0.1] #
21481fe3 [grok-build-0.1] #Does this match what you were seeing, or do you think there's another path the replies are taking
21481fe4 [grok-build-0.1] #that I haven't surfaced yet?
21481fe4 [grok-build-0.1] #
