21482371 [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.115 355.2KB 8.48s
21482371 [grok-build-0.1] #• Good direction.*
21482372 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482372 [grok-build-0.1] #Adding tagging from the main relay makes sense given the constraints we've established (relay owns
21482372 [grok-build-0.1] #long-lived history, bots are shorter-lived interfaces, and bots should only act on @mentions).
21482372 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482373 [grok-build-0.1] #What "tagging from the relay" would mean
21482373 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482373 [grok-build-0.1] #Instead of the relay doing a pure broadcast to every connected bot, it would attach a recipient
21482373 [grok-build-0.1] #tag (or set of tags) when it sends messages outward over TCP.
21482374 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482374 [grok-build-0.1] #This shifts responsibility for routing decisions to the relay, where the context and history live.
21482374 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482374 [grok-build-0.1] #Key things this enables
21482375 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482375 [grok-build-0.1] #- The relay can decide which specific bot should handle a message, based on the incoming
21482375 [grok-build-0.1] #@mention.
21482375 [grok-build-0.1] #- Bots can become passive listeners: they only process and emit to Discord if the tag matches their
21482376 [grok-build-0.1] #identity.
21482376 [grok-build-0.1] #- It creates a cleaner separation:
21482376 [grok-build-0.1] #  - Relay = decision maker + state owner
21482376 [grok-build-0.1] #  - Bots = delivery mechanism + identity filter
21482377 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482377 [grok-build-0.1] #Areas we should discuss before going further
21482377 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482377 [grok-build-0.1] #Here are the main architectural questions this raises:
21482378 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482378 [grok-build-0.1] #1. What form should the tag take?
21482378 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Simple bot name (`"sloppy"`, `"riff"`, `"foggy"`)?
21482378 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Something more structured (e.g. a target field, a list of targets, or a special "all" value)?
21482379 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Should system-generated messages (tool results, status, model replies) be tagged differently
21482379 [grok-build-0.1] #from user-triggered replies?
21482379 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482379 [grok-build-0.1] #2. How does the relay know who to tag?
2148237a [grok-build-0.1] #   - Primarily driven by the @mention in the original Discord message (passed up from the bot)?
2148237a [grok-build-0.1] #   - Does the relay need to maintain a mapping of bot names to active connections?
2148237a [grok-build-0.1] #   - What happens if the mentioned bot is not currently connected?
2148237a [grok-build-0.1] #
2148237b [grok-build-0.1] #3. Default / fallback behavior
2148237b [grok-build-0.1] #   - If a message arrives with no @mention (even if we later decide to still forward some of them
2148237b [grok-build-0.1] #for history), what tag does the relay use?
2148237b [grok-build-0.1] #   - Should there be a concept of a "default" or "primary" bot?
2148237c [grok-build-0.1] #
2148237c [grok-build-0.1] #4. Commands
2148237c [grok-build-0.1] #   - Should commands also carry a target tag, or are they treated as system-wide?
2148237c [grok-build-0.1] #   - If a command comes in via `@sloppy /reset`, should the tag be used to decide which bot
2148237d [grok-build-0.1] #acknowledges the result?
2148237d [grok-build-0.1] #
2148237d [grok-build-0.1] #5. Messages originating in the relay
2148237d [grok-build-0.1] #   - Things like model completions, tool call results, or internal announcements still need to
2148237e [grok-build-0.1] #reach Discord.
2148237e [grok-build-0.1] #   - Should these be tagged to a specific bot, or is there a different path/mechanism for
2148237e [grok-build-0.1] #"relay-initiated" output?
2148237f [grok-build-0.1] #
2148237f [grok-build-0.1] #6. Multiple targets
2148237f [grok-build-0.1] #   - Do we need to support tagging a message to more than one bot at once (if someone @mentions two
2148237f [grok-build-0.1] #bots)?
21482380 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482380 [grok-build-0.1] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
21482380 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482380 [grok-build-0.1] #This approach feels like it reduces the "obfuscation" you mentioned earlier, because the relay
21482381 [grok-build-0.1] #becomes explicit about intent when it emits messages.
21482381 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482381 [grok-build-0.1] #Quick questions to sharpen this
21482381 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482382 [grok-build-0.1] #- Do you want the tagging to be strict (if the tag doesn't match the bot, it drops the
21482382 [grok-build-0.1] #message completely)?
21482382 [grok-build-0.1] #- Should the relay also tag incoming messages from bots (so it knows which bot originated a
21482382 [grok-build-0.1] #user message or command)?
21482383 [grok-build-0.1] #- Are you thinking of the tag as something that travels with every message the relay sends
21482383 [grok-build-0.1] #outward, or only certain types?
21482383 [grok-build-0.1] #
21482383 [grok-build-0.1] #Let me know how you want to narrow this down. We can keep refining the flow before any
21482384 [grok-build-0.1] #implementation details.
21482384 [grok-build-0.1] #
