214a728c [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.071 224.6KB 18.85s
214a728d [grok-build-0.1] #• No, you cannot give `getvar()` a default argument.*
214a728d [grok-build-0.1] #
214a728d [grok-build-0.1] #This is C11 (as set in your CMakeLists.txt). Default arguments are a C++-only feature and do not
214a728d [grok-build-0.1] #exist in any version of C.
214a728d [grok-build-0.1] #
214a728d [grok-build-0.1] #What is actually happening with getvar()
214a728e [grok-build-0.1] #
214a728e [grok-build-0.1] #The current issue is almost certainly not a call site that is missing an argument. The real
214a728e [grok-build-0.1] #problem is this line inside `nxtins()`:
214a728e [grok-build-0.1] #
214a728f [grok-build-0.1] #
214a728f [grok-build-0.1] #short argcount,
214a728f [grok-build-0.1] #      ts1, getvar();   /* temp for signed operations */
214a7290 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a7290 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a7290 [grok-build-0.1] #In the original 1986 K&R style, this was a (very old) way of mixing a variable declaration with a
214a7290 [grok-build-0.1] #forward function declaration in the same statement. Modern C (especially C11) no longer allows this
214a7290 [grok-build-0.1] #syntax. The compiler now treats the `getvar();` part as invalid in a variable declaration context.
214a7291 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a7291 [grok-build-0.1] #This is why you're seeing an error attached to `getvar()` right now.
214a7291 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a7291 [grok-build-0.1] #Next steps (discussion only)
214a7292 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a7292 [grok-build-0.1] #- The line needs to be split so that `ts1` is just a plain local variable and any declaration for
214a7292 [grok-build-0.1] #`getvar()` lives elsewhere (preferably as a proper prototype at the top of the file, which you
214a7292 [grok-build-0.1] #already started doing).
214a7293 [grok-build-0.1] #- Adding a default argument is not the solution and will not compile.
214a7293 [grok-build-0.1] #- Once this declaration line is cleaned up, the next error is likely to be either:
214a7293 [grok-build-0.1] #  - another old-style function definition that still lacks an explicit return type, or
214a7294 [grok-build-0.1] #  - a missing declaration for one of the machine-dependent functions (because of the `#ifdef UNIX`
214a7294 [grok-build-0.1] #guards).
214a7294 [grok-build-0.1] #
