214a8b24 [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.099 333.0KB 17.24s
214a8b24 [grok-build-0.1] #• Yes, let's talk about `putvar` and return values.*
214a8b24 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b24 [grok-build-0.1] #`putvar` is a setter — its only job is to store a value somewhere (global, local, or top of
214a8b25 [grok-build-0.1] #stack). It is not supposed to return anything to the caller. That is why you see bare
214a8b25 [grok-build-0.1] #`return;` statements inside it: they just mean “exit the function now, I have finished the set
214a8b25 [grok-build-0.1] #operation”.
214a8b25 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b25 [grok-build-0.1] #The current confusion
214a8b25 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b26 [grok-build-0.1] #Because the function is still written in the old style (`putvar(var, value)` with no return type in
214a8b26 [grok-build-0.1] #front), the compiler treats it as if it returns an `int`. This creates a mismatch in the compiler’s
214a8b26 [grok-build-0.1] #mind between “this function has no return value” (the `return;` lines) and “this function is
214a8b26 [grok-build-0.1] #supposed to return an int”.
214a8b26 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b27 [grok-build-0.1] #That is almost certainly what is triggering the warning or error you are seeing about return
214a8b27 [grok-build-0.1] #values.
214a8b27 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b27 [grok-build-0.1] #Additional problem in the stack case
214a8b28 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b28 [grok-build-0.1] #There is also a clear logic bug in the branch that handles the stack (when `var` is 0). In that
214a8b28 [grok-build-0.1] #branch the code is using the variable number instead of the actual value you want to store. This
214a8b28 [grok-build-0.1] #means the value parameter is effectively being ignored for top-of-stack stores. That is likely
214a8b29 [grok-build-0.1] #adding to your confusion about “return values”.
214a8b29 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b29 [grok-build-0.1] #What needs to happen conceptually
214a8b29 [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b2a [grok-build-0.1] #1. The function should be declared as returning nothing.
214a8b2a [grok-build-0.1] #2. The prototype (if you add one) and the definition must agree on that.
214a8b2a [grok-build-0.1] #3. The stack-top assignment needs to actually use the `value` that was passed in.
214a8b2a [grok-build-0.1] #
214a8b2a [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like to discuss the cleanest way to mark the function as returning nothing (both at the
214a8b2b [grok-build-0.1] #prototype level and at the definition level), or shall we focus first on why the stack case is
214a8b2b [grok-build-0.1] #mishandling the value that is being “put”?
214a8b2b [grok-build-0.1] #
