215d7cc8 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a SIGQUIT in windows?
215d7d3b [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.034 106.3KB 5.96s
215d7d3b [grok-build-0.1] #• No, Windows does not* have `SIGQUIT`.
215d7d3b [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d3b [grok-build-0.1] #Details:
215d7d3c [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d3c [grok-build-0.1] #- `SIGQUIT` is a POSIX/Unix signal (usually signal 3).
215d7d3c [grok-build-0.1] #- On Unix it is typically triggered by `Ctrl+\`.
215d7d3c [grok-build-0.1] #- Windows has very limited signal support. The standard `signal()` in `<signal.h>` only properly
215d7d3c [grok-build-0.1] #supports these:
215d7d3c [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d3d [grok-build-0.1] #╭──────────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────╮
215d7d3d [grok-build-0.1] #│  Signal      │  Windows support   │  Notes        │
215d7d3d [grok-build-0.1] #├──────────────┼────────────────────┼───────────────┤
215d7d3d [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGINT`    │  Yes               │  Ctrl+C       │
215d7d3d [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGTERM`   │  Limited           │  -            │
215d7d3e [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGABRT`   │  Yes               │  -            │
215d7d3e [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGFPE`    │  Yes               │  -            │
215d7d3e [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGILL`    │  Yes               │  -            │
215d7d3e [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGSEGV`   │  Yes               │  -            │
215d7d3f [grok-build-0.1] #│  `SIGQUIT`   │  **No**            │  Not defined  │
215d7d3f [grok-build-0.1] #╰──────────────┴────────────────────┴───────────────╯
215d7d3f [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d3f [grok-build-0.1] #If you compile the original line:
215d7d40 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d40 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d40 [grok-build-0.1] #signal(SIGQUIT, z_exit);
215d7d40 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d41 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d41 [grok-build-0.1] #on Windows (even with MinGW or MSVC), you will get an error because `SIGQUIT` is not defined.
215d7d41 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d41 [grok-build-0.1] #Recommended approach for your modernization
215d7d41 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d42 [grok-build-0.1] #Since you're aiming for platform-agnostic modern C, here’s the cleanest way to handle it:
215d7d42 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d42 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d42 [grok-build-0.1] ##ifdef _WIN32
215d7d42 [grok-build-0.1] #    signal(SIGINT, z_exit);
215d7d42 [grok-build-0.1] #    /* SIGQUIT does not exist on Windows */
215d7d43 [grok-build-0.1] ##else
215d7d43 [grok-build-0.1] #    signal(SIGINT, z_exit);
215d7d43 [grok-build-0.1] #    signal(SIGQUIT, z_exit);
215d7d43 [grok-build-0.1] ##endif
215d7d44 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d44 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d44 [grok-build-0.1] #Or, if you want it a bit more structured, you can wrap it in a helper:
215d7d44 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d44 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d45 [grok-build-0.1] #static void setup_signals(void)
215d7d45 [grok-build-0.1] #{
215d7d45 [grok-build-0.1] #    signal(SIGINT, z_exit);
215d7d46 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d46 [grok-build-0.1] ##ifndef _WIN32
215d7d46 [grok-build-0.1] #    signal(SIGQUIT, z_exit);
215d7d46 [grok-build-0.1] ##endif
215d7d46 [grok-build-0.1] #}
215d7d47 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d47 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d47 [grok-build-0.1] #Bonus (better long-term option)
215d7d47 [grok-build-0.1] #
215d7d48 [grok-build-0.1] #For Windows console apps, many people prefer `SetConsoleCtrlHandler` over `signal()` because it
215d7d48 [grok-build-0.1] #gives more control. If you want to go that route later, I can show you a portable abstraction.
215d7d48 [grok-build-0.1] #
