215e088c [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.114 393.2KB 18.46s
215e088c [grok-build-0.1] #• Diagnosis of the error*
215e088c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088c [grok-build-0.1] #The crash comes from this line in `dip.c`:
215e088c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088d [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088d [grok-build-0.1] #lseek(gamechn, offset, 0);
215e088d [grok-build-0.1] #if (read(gamechn, loc, BLKSIZ) == 0) THEN
215e088e [grok-build-0.1] #    fatal("Get block failed");
215e088e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088e [grok-build-0.1] #(See: `getblk()` called from `memini()` for block 0, and again for the picture block.)
215e088e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088e [grok-build-0.1] #The `open(gamfile, RDONLY)` succeeded (otherwise you would have seen "Failed to open game file"),
215e088f [grok-build-0.1] #but the first 512-byte read returned 0 bytes.
215e088f [grok-build-0.1] #
215e088f [grok-build-0.1] #Most likely causes (in order)
215e088f [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0890 [grok-build-0.1] #1. `..\foo.dat` is empty or too small
215e0890 [grok-build-0.1] #   A valid Z-machine story file (even a tiny test) must be at least 512 bytes for the first block.
215e0890 [grok-build-0.1] #   Run from the `bin` directory:
215e0890 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0890 [grok-build-0.1] #   dir ..\foo.dat
215e0891 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0891 [grok-build-0.1] #   If it shows 0 bytes (or doesn't exist), that's the problem.
215e0891 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0892 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Windows binary-mode problem (very common with this old code)
215e0892 [grok-build-0.1] #   The code does:
215e0892 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0892 [grok-build-0.1] #   gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY);   // RDONLY == 0
215e0893 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0893 [grok-build-0.1] #   On Windows (especially with MinGW/MSVC), `open()` defaults to text mode. This can cause:
215e0893 [grok-build-0.1] #   - CR/LF translation
215e0893 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Early EOF on binary data
215e0894 [grok-build-0.1] #   - Short or zero-byte reads for the first block
215e0894 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0894 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Missing error checking on `lseek`
215e0894 [grok-build-0.1] #   `lseek` can fail silently here; the code never checks its return value.
215e0895 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0895 [grok-build-0.1] #4. Less likely: the file is a valid story file but the image file header logic (`ibase =
215e0895 [grok-build-0.1] #GTVWRD(PLENTH) + IFUDGE`) is pointing at garbage because the first block read was already
215e0895 [grok-build-0.1] #corrupted.
215e0896 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0896 [grok-build-0.1] #Quick checks
215e0896 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0896 [grok-build-0.1] #From `C:\nitrologic\entheogen\dip\bin`:
215e0897 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0897 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0897 [grok-build-0.1] #dir ..\foo.dat
215e0897 [grok-build-0.1] #type ..\foo.dat | more
215e0897 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0898 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0898 [grok-build-0.1] #If `foo.dat` is a placeholder you just created, it will be empty and will always fail here.
215e0898 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0898 [grok-build-0.1] #Recommended fixes (if you want to patch the source)
215e0899 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0899 [grok-build-0.1] #In `dip.c`, change the open in `sysini()`:
215e0899 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0899 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089a [grok-build-0.1] ##ifdef _WIN32
215e089a [grok-build-0.1] #    gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY | O_BINARY);
215e089a [grok-build-0.1] ##else
215e089a [grok-build-0.1] #    gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY);
215e089a [grok-build-0.1] ##endif
215e089b [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089b [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089b [grok-build-0.1] #Also add a size check and better diagnostics in `getblk()`:
215e089b [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089c [grok-build-0.1] #if (lseek(gamechn, offset, 0) == -1) {
215e089c [grok-build-0.1] #    printf("lseek failed for block %d\n", block);
215e089c [grok-build-0.1] #    fatal("Get block failed");
215e089d [grok-build-0.1] #}
215e089d [grok-build-0.1] #if (read(gamechn, loc, BLKSIZ) != BLKSIZ) {
215e089d [grok-build-0.1] #    printf("read returned short/zero for block %d (file too small?)\n", block);
215e089e [grok-build-0.1] #    fatal("Get block failed");
215e089e [grok-build-0.1] #}
215e089e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089f [grok-build-0.1] #You may also need to define `O_BINARY` or include `<fcntl.h>` properly on Windows.
215e089f [grok-build-0.1] #
215e089f [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like me to:
215e089f [grok-build-0.1] #- Show the exact minimal patch set for Windows?
215e089f [grok-build-0.1] #- Add a file-size sanity check in `memini()`?
215e08a0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Suggest how to generate a minimal valid `foo.dat` for testing?
215e08a0 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e08a0 [grok-build-0.1] #Just say the word.
215e08a0 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e08a1 [roha] [TODO] annotateCode dip-get-block-failed-debug Analysis of "Fatal error: Get block failed" in DIP. Root cause in getblk(): read(gamechn, loc, BLKSIZ)==0 after successful open. Common on Windows: empty foo.dat, missing O_BINARY in open(), unchecked lseek(), or invalid Z-machine story file. Suggests adding O_BINARY|O_RDONLY, error checks on lseek/read, and file size validation.
215e0e93 [nitro@ryzen5] is "rb" faild for second open argument
215e0f88 [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.3 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪣  0.7° $0.115 402.8KB 5.09s
215e0f88 [grok-build-0.1] #• No.*
215e0f88 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f88 [grok-build-0.1] #`"rb"` is not valid for the second argument to `open()`.
215e0f88 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f89 [grok-build-0.1] #Why?
215e0f89 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f89 [grok-build-0.1] #- `open()` (the function used in this code) is the low-level file function.
215e0f8a [grok-build-0.1] #  - Second argument must be an integer (bit flags).
215e0f8a [grok-build-0.1] #  - Examples: `O_RDONLY`, `O_RDWR`, `O_BINARY`, etc.
215e0f8a [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8a [grok-build-0.1] #- `"rb"` is a mode string used only with the higher-level `fopen()`:
215e0f8b [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8b [grok-build-0.1] #  FILE *f = fopen("foo.dat", "rb");   // correct for fopen
215e0f8b [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8b [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8b [grok-build-0.1] #In `dip.c` you have:
215e0f8c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8c [grok-build-0.1] #gamechn = open(gamfile, RDONLY);     // RDONLY == 0
215e0f8c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8c [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8d [grok-build-0.1] #This is using `open()` + `read()` + `lseek()`, not `fopen()`.
215e0f8d [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8d [grok-build-0.1] #Correct fix for your Windows build
215e0f8e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8e [grok-build-0.1] #You need to open the file in binary mode. Change the definition or the call.
215e0f8e [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8e [grok-build-0.1] #• Best place to fix it* is near the top of `dip.c` or in `dipdefs.h`:
215e0f8f [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8f [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f8f [grok-build-0.1] ##ifdef _WIN32
215e0f8f [grok-build-0.1] #    #include <fcntl.h>
215e0f90 [grok-build-0.1] #    #define RDONLY (O_RDONLY | O_BINARY)
215e0f90 [grok-build-0.1] ##else
215e0f90 [grok-build-0.1] #    #define RDONLY O_RDONLY
215e0f90 [grok-build-0.1] ##endif
215e0f90 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f91 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f91 [grok-build-0.1] #Or just change the `open()` call in `sysini()`:
215e0f91 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f91 [grok-build-0.1] #
215e0f92 [grok-build-0.1] ##ifdef _WIN32
215e0f92 [grok-build-0.1] #    gamechn = open(gamfile, O_RDONLY | O_BINARY);
215e0f92 [grok-build-0.1] ##else
