215e41e5 [grok-build-0.1] #Quick diagnosis (no code changes yet)
215e41e6 [grok-build-0.1] #- Graphics path is the main suspect: All drawing goes through `md_drawblock()` →
215e41e6 [grok-build-0.1] #`altscreen` (the alternate buffer). The only place that should copy it to the real screen is
215e41e6 [grok-build-0.1] #`md_screen_update()`. In the non-UNIX build (`#ifndef HAS_URDATA`) that function is an empty stub,
215e41e6 [grok-build-0.1] #so nothing ever appears.
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Text path is also limited: The only `printf`/`putchar` calls are for errors, prompts
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #(save/restore), and the ANSI sequences in `cls()`/`locate()`. The actual game logic only uses a
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #very small subset of opcodes (arithmetic, calls, `OPSHOWI`/`OPSHOWN`/`OPCLEAR`, `OPINPUT`, etc.).
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #There are no print-string, print-char, or status-line opcodes implemented. If the game file
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #is purely graphics-oriented this may be intentional, but it means you will see almost nothing
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #unless the game actually emits `showi`/`clear` calls.
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #- Windows console issues:
215e41e7 [grok-build-0.1] #  - ANSI escapes (`\033[2J` etc.) are not guaranteed in the classic cmd.exe without extra setup.
215e41e8 [grok-build-0.1] #  - `md_ttyini()` just returns a handle; it does not put the console into raw/non-blocking mode.
215e41e8 [grok-build-0.1] #  - `md_input()` relies on `getchar()` returning 0xFF for "no input", which doesn't happen with the
215e41e8 [grok-build-0.1] #default blocking stdio on Windows.
215e41e8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Other notes: The original code was written for an AT&T 7300 (tam.h, urdata ioctl, etc.).
