215ee99b [glm-5.2] #Here's what this version does differently:
215ee99b [glm-5.2] #
215ee99b [glm-5.2] #How it works
215ee99b [glm-5.2] #
215ee99b [glm-5.2] #Each 8×8 pixel block is split into a 2×2 grid of 4×4 sub-regions:
215ee99b [glm-5.2] #
215ee99c [glm-5.2] #
215ee99c [glm-5.2] #┌──┬──┐
215ee99c [glm-5.2] #│..│::│   Each sub-region counts how many of its 16 pixels
215ee99c [glm-5.2] #├──┼──┤   are set, then picks a character by density:
215ee99c [glm-5.2] #│++│oo│
215ee99d [glm-5.2] #└──┴──┘   ' ' = 0 pixels    '+' = 6-9
215ee99d [glm-5.2] #          '.' = 1-2 pixels   'o' = 10-13
215ee99d [glm-5.2] #          ':' = 3-5 pixels   '#' = 14-16
215ee99e [glm-5.2] #
215ee99e [glm-5.2] #
215ee99e [glm-5.2] #Key points
215ee99e [glm-5.2] #
215ee99f [glm-5.2] #- 80 columns × 48 lines total output (fits standard terminal)
215ee99f [glm-5.2] #- Line buffer — each text line is built in `linebuf[]` before output, so it's one `fputs`
215ee99f [glm-5.2] #per line instead of per-character `putchar`
215ee99f [glm-5.2] #- Partial updates — `locate()` positions at `locx * 2 + 1` so icon-only redraws land in the
215ee9a0 [glm-5.2] #right spot
215ee9a0 [glm-5.2] #- `density_to_char()` is a named reusable function (not inlined)
215ee9a0 [glm-5.2] #- Reads pixel bits from `altscreen` using the `rev_byte` bit layout: pixel `i` lives at bit `i*2`
215ee9a0 [glm-5.2] #in each `unsigned short`
215ee9a1 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a1 [glm-5.2] #Pixel extraction
215ee9a1 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a1 [glm-5.2] #The `rev_byte()` function doubles each bit, so to read pixel `i` (0=leftmost) from a short:
215ee9a2 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a2 [glm-5.2] #(sv >> (i * 2)) & 1
215ee9a2 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a3 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a3 [glm-5.2] #The sub-region indexing `px  2 + subx  4` covers:
215ee9a3 [glm-5.2] #- `subx=0`: pixels 0–3 (left half)
215ee9a3 [glm-5.2] #- `subx=1`: pixels 4–7 (right half)
215ee9a3 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a4 [glm-5.2] #Drop it in replacing your current `#ifndef HAS_URDATA` version and you should see shapes instead of
215ee9a4 [glm-5.2] #solid walls. Let me know how it looks.
215ee9a4 [glm-5.2] #
215ee9a4 [glm-5.2] #Here's a version that renders each 8×8 block as 2 chars wide × 2 lines tall (80×48
215ee9a5 [glm-5.2] #display), using density characters built up in a line buffer:
215ee9a5 [glm-5.2] #
