2188df4b [nitro@ryzen5] what is phoff and shoff
2188e022 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣 0.7° $0.024 70.95KB 13.08s
2188e023 [glm-5.2] #They're file offsets — byte positions within the ELF file where the two header tables begin.
2188e023 [glm-5.2] #
2188e023 [glm-5.2] #`e_phoff` — Program Header Table Offset
2188e023 [glm-5.2] #- Tells the loader where to find the program headers (segment descriptors).
2188e024 [glm-5.2] #- In `vidbot1.elf` this is `0x34` = 52, which is immediately after the 52-byte ELF header.
2188e024 [glm-5.2] #- Each entry describes a segment — a contiguous chunk of the file that gets mapped into
2188e024 [glm-5.2] #memory at load time. For firmware, that's typically flash (`.text`, `.rodata`) and RAM (`.data`,
2188e024 [glm-5.2] #`.bss`).
2188e025 [glm-5.2] #- The loader reads `e_phnum` entries of `e_phentsize` bytes each, starting at this offset.
2188e025 [glm-5.2] #
2188e025 [glm-5.2] #`e_shoff` — Section Header Table Offset
2188e025 [glm-5.2] #- Tells tools like `readelf`/`elfutil` where to find the section headers.
2188e026 [glm-5.2] #- In `vidbot1.elf` this is `0x3fca8` = 261,032, near the end of the file.
2188e026 [glm-5.2] #- Each entry describes a section — a named region like `.text`, `.data`, `.bss`, `.symtab`,
2188e026 [glm-5.2] #`.shstrtab`.
2188e026 [glm-5.2] #- The section table is primarily for linkers and debuggers, not the runtime loader. Many
2188e027 [glm-5.2] #embedded loaders ignore it entirely.
2188e027 [glm-5.2] #- You read `e_shnum` entries of `e_shentsize` bytes each, starting at this offset.
2188e027 [glm-5.2] #
2188e027 [glm-5.2] #Simple diagram
2188e028 [glm-5.2] #
2188e028 [glm-5.2] #
2188e028 [glm-5.2] #File offset
2188e028 [glm-5.2] #0x00000 ┌─────────────────────┐
2188e029 [glm-5.2] #        │  ELF header (52 B)  │
2188e029 [glm-5.2] #0x00034 ├─────────────────────┤  ← e_phoff
2188e029 [glm-5.2] #        │  Program headers    │  (8 × e_phentsize)
2188e029 [glm-5.2] #        │  ...                │
2188e02a [glm-5.2] #        │  (file data)        │
2188e02a [glm-5.2] #0x3fca8 ├─────────────────────┤  ← e_shoff
2188e02a [glm-5.2] #        │  Section headers    │  (32 × e_shentsize)
2188e02a [glm-5.2] #        └─────────────────────┘
2188e02b [glm-5.2] #
2188e02b [glm-5.2] #
2188e02b [glm-5.2] #So `e_phoff` points at the load map (what goes in memory), and `e_shoff` points at the
2188e02c [glm-5.2] #section map (what's in the file for tooling).
2188e02c [glm-5.2] #
2188e297 [nitro@ryzen5] should i use seek to load or just offsets in memory?
2188e38e [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪣 0.7° $0.026 72.87KB 14.88s
2188e38e [glm-5.2] #Good question. Both approaches work — here are the trade-offs:
2188e38e [glm-5.2] #
2188e38e [glm-5.2] #Option A: Seek-based (current approach)
2188e38e [glm-5.2] #Each function opens the file and `seekg` to the offset.
2188e38f [glm-5.2] #
2188e38f [glm-5.2] #
2188e38f [glm-5.2] #std::ifstream file(path, std::ios::binary);
2188e390 [glm-5.2] #file.seekg(hdr.e_phoff);
2188e390 [glm-5.2] #file.read(reinterpret_cast<char*>(phdrs.data()), count);
2188e390 [glm-5.2] #
2188e390 [glm-5.2] #
2188e391 [glm-5.2] #• Pros:* Minimal memory — only reads what you need.
2188e391 [glm-5.2] #• Cons:* Opens the file 4+ times (header, phdrs, shdrs, strtab). Each open/seek has small
2188e391 [glm-5.2] #overhead. More error handling.
2188e391 [glm-5.2] #
2188e392 [glm-5.2] #Option B: Load whole file into memory once
2188e392 [glm-5.2] #Read the entire file into a `std::vector<uint8_t>` buffer, then index directly with offsets.
2188e392 [glm-5.2] #
2188e392 [glm-5.2] #
2188e393 [glm-5.2] #std::vector<uint8_t> buf;
2188e393 [glm-5.2] #// read entire file into buf once
2188e393 [glm-5.2] #
2188e393 [glm-5.2] #auto* hdr = reinterpret_cast<struct elf32*>(buf.data());
2188e394 [glm-5.2] #auto* phdrs = reinterpret_cast<struct elf32_phdr*>(buf.data() + hdr->e_phoff);
2188e394 [glm-5.2] #auto* shdrs = reinterpret_cast<struct elf32_shdr*>(buf.data() + hdr->e_shoff);
2188e394 [glm-5.2] #const char* strtab = reinterpret_cast<const char*>(buf.data() + shdrs[hdr->e_shstrndx].sh_offset);
2188e394 [glm-5.2] #
2188e395 [glm-5.2] #
2188e395 [glm-5.2] #• Pros:* One file open, no seeks, pointer math is fast and simple. No repeated I/O. Natural
2188e395 [glm-5.2] #for a small firmware ELF (~260KB).
2188e395 [glm-5.2] #• Cons:* Reads the whole file even if you only want the header. For huge files that's
2188e396 [glm-5.2] #wasteful, but for embedded binaries it's negligible.
2188e396 [glm-5.2] #
2188e396 [glm-5.2] #Recommendation
2188e396 [glm-5.2] #
2188e397 [glm-5.2] #For `vidbot1.elf` at ~260KB, Option B is cleaner — one read, then everything is just
2188e397 [glm-5.2] #pointer arithmetic off the buffer. It also eliminates the repeated `ifstream` opens and makes the
2188e397 [glm-5.2] #code easier to follow.
2188e397 [glm-5.2] #
2188e398 [glm-5.2] #Want me to refactor `elfutil.cpp` to use the buffer approach?
2188e398 [glm-5.2] #
