21896f71 [glm-5.2] #Here are a few things to keep in mind as you move forward with the buffer approach:
21896f71 [glm-5.2] #
21896f71 [glm-5.2] #1. The Pointer Math Quirk
21896f71 [glm-5.2] #Because your `load_elf32` function reads the 52-byte header into `hdr` and then reads the
21896f72 [glm-5.2] #rest of the file into the `content` vector, your buffer starts at file offset 52.
21896f72 [glm-5.2] #You've already accounted for this perfectly with:
21896f72 [glm-5.2] #`content.data() + (elf32header.e_phoff - sizeof(elf32))`
21896f72 [glm-5.2] #Just remember that any offset you pull from the ELF metadata (like `sh_offset` inside a section
21896f72 [glm-5.2] #header) will also need `sizeof(elf32)` subtracted from it if you want to index it into that
21896f73 [glm-5.2] #`content` buffer.
21896f73 [glm-5.2] #
21896f73 [glm-5.2] #2. Un-commenting the Structs
21896f73 [glm-5.2] #When you bring `elf32_phdr` and `elf32_shdr` back out of the comment block, you can cast those `ph`
21896f73 [glm-5.2] #and `sh` pointers directly to them.
21896f74 [glm-5.2] #Because your `content` vector holds `char`s, you'll be casting from `const char` to `struct
21896f74 [glm-5.2] #elf32_phdr`.
21896f74 [glm-5.2] #
21896f74 [glm-5.2] #3. Iterating through headers
21896f74 [glm-5.2] #Once you have the pointer to the first program header, you can step through them. Since
21896f74 [glm-5.2] #`e_phentsize` (and `e_shentsize`) are in the header, you can just add that byte size to your
21896f75 [glm-5.2] #pointer to get to the next entry. In standard ELF32, the entry size is exactly the size of the
21896f75 [glm-5.2] #struct, but using the `entsize` field from the header is the robust way to do it.
21896f75 [glm-5.2] #
21896f75 [glm-5.2] #4. Enums vs. Maps
21896f75 [glm-5.2] #If you decide to convert the `PT_` and `SHT_` constants to enums like you did with `etype`,
21896f75 [glm-5.2] #remember that `p_type` has some weird values (like `0x6474e551` for GNU_EH_FRAME) and `sh_type` can
21896f76 [glm-5.2] #have target-specific values (like `0x70000001` for ARM attributes). A `std::map<uint32_t,
21896f76 [glm-5.2] #std::string>` is usually safer for those two than a sequential vector, since the values aren't 0,
21896f76 [glm-5.2] #1, 2, 3...
