2189b488 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.048 124.3KB 15.38s
2189b488 [glm-5.2] #`e_phnum` stands for Program Header Number. It tells you how many Program Headers
2189b488 [glm-5.2] #(segments) are in the file. In your `vidbot1.elf`, this is `8`.
2189b488 [glm-5.2] #
2189b488 [glm-5.2] #To understand the difference between sections (`e_shnum`) and segments (`e_phnum`), here is the
2189b489 [glm-5.2] #easiest way to think about it:
2189b489 [glm-5.2] #
2189b489 [glm-5.2] #• Sections (sh) are for the Linker and Debugger. They care about the tiny, logical pieces
2189b48a [glm-5.2] #of the file: `.text`, `.rodata`, `.bss`, `.symtab`, `.debug_info`.
2189b48a [glm-5.2] #• Segments (ph) are for the OS/Loader. When the firmware boots, the loader doesn't care
2189b48a [glm-5.2] #about individual section names. It just says, "Give me a big chunk of bytes from the file, put it
2189b48a [glm-5.2] #at memory address X, and make it Read-Only." A segment is just a grouping of one or more sections.
2189b48b [glm-5.2] #
2189b48b [glm-5.2] #For example, the loader will take the `.text` and `.rodata` sections, group them together into a
2189b48b [glm-5.2] #single `PT_LOAD` segment, and map it to `0x10000000` (Flash) with Read/Execute permissions. Then it
2189b48b [glm-5.2] #will take `.data` and `.bss`, group them into another `PT_LOAD` segment, and map it to RAM with
2189b48c [glm-5.2] #Read/Write permissions.
2189b48c [glm-5.2] #
2189b48c [glm-5.2] #Since this is an RP2040 firmware, your 8 program headers (`ph0` through `ph7`) likely consist of:
2189b48d [glm-5.2] #1.  `PT_LOAD` - The Flash segment (code + read-only data).
2189b48d [glm-5.2] #2.  `PT_LOAD` - The RAM segment (initialized data).
2189b48d [glm-5.2] #3.  `PT_LOAD` - Another RAM segment (maybe `.data` vs `.bss` splitting).
2189b48d [glm-5.2] #4.  `PT_ARM_EXIDX` - ARM exception unwinding info.
2189b48e [glm-5.2] #5.  `PT_NOTE` - Build IDs or binary info metadata.
2189b48e [glm-5.2] #6.  A couple of others (like `PT_PHDR` or `PT_TLS`).
2189b48e [glm-5.2] #
2189b48e [glm-5.2] #You already have the pointer to them in your code:
2189b48f [glm-5.2] #`const char *ph = content.data()+(elf32header.e_phoff-sizeof(elf32));`
2189b48f [glm-5.2] #
2189b48f [glm-5.2] #If you un-comment `struct elf32_phdr` in your header file, you can cast that `ph` pointer just like
2189b48f [glm-5.2] #you did with `sh` and print out `p_type`, `p_vaddr` (where it goes in memory), and `p_memsz`!
2189b48f [glm-5.2] #
