2189c666 [glm-5.2] #Because your output is in decimal, the numbers look a bit wild, but if we convert them to hex, the
2189c666 [glm-5.2] #entire memory map of the RP2040 reveals itself perfectly.
2189c667 [glm-5.2] #
2189c667 [glm-5.2] #Here are your segments translated to hex:
2189c667 [glm-5.2] #
2189c667 [glm-5.2] #╭───────────┬────────────────┬────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2189c668 [glm-5.2] #│  Segment  │  Type (Hex)    │  vaddr (Hex)   │  Interpretation                                                                                                         │
2189c668 [glm-5.2] #├───────────┼────────────────┼────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2189c668 [glm-5.2] #│  :---     │  :---          │  :---          │  :---                                                                                                                   │
2189c669 [glm-5.2] #│  **p0**   │  `0x70000001`  │  `0x100014D4`  │  **PT_ARM_EXIDX**. ARM exception unwinding tables, living in Flash.                                                     │
2189c669 [glm-5.2] #│  **p1**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x10000000`  │  **Main Flash Code**. Starts at the very beginning of XIP Flash (`0x10000000`). This holds your `.text` and `.rodata`.  │
2189c669 [glm-5.2] #│  **p2**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x200001F0`  │  **RAM Data**. Starts in SRAM at `0x200001F0`. This is your `.data` section.                                            │
2189c669 [glm-5.2] #│  **p3**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x100016C0`  │  **Flash Metadata**. Up in Flash, likely `.binary_info`.                                                                │
2189c66a [glm-5.2] #│  **p4**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20000398`  │  **RAM BSS/Heap**. Higher up in SRAM.                                                                                   │
2189c66a [glm-5.2] #│  **p5**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20000594`  │  **RAM Scratch/Stack**. Even higher in SRAM.                                                                            │
2189c66a [glm-5.2] #│  **p6**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20000000`  │  **RAM Vectors**. Starts at the absolute bottom of SRAM (`0x20000000`). This is your `.ram_vector_table`.               │
2189c66a [glm-5.2] #│  **p7**   │  `0x1` (LOAD)  │  `0x20082000`  │  **Stack Pointer!** `0x20082000` is the exact top of the RP2040's SRAM. The stack grows downwards from here.            │
2189c66a [glm-5.2] #╰───────────┴────────────────┴────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2189c66b [glm-5.2] #
2189c66b [glm-5.2] #What this tells us
2189c66b [glm-5.2] #The loader uses these `p_vaddr` (Virtual Address) values to know exactly where to copy things.
2189c66b [glm-5.2] #• It takes `p1`, copies a chunk of the file to `0x10000000` (but since it's execute-in-place flash,
2189c66c [glm-5.2] #it actually just leaves it there).
2189c66c [glm-5.2] #• It takes `p2`, copies initialized variables from Flash to `0x200001F0` in RAM.
2189c66c [glm-5.2] #• It sees `p7` and knows that the stack pointer needs to be initialized to `0x20082000`.
2189c66c [glm-5.2] #
2189c66d [glm-5.2] #You now have a fully functioning memory map parser!
2189c66d [glm-5.2] #
2189c66d [glm-5.2] #If you wanted to take it one step further, printing `p_memsz` and `p_filesz` next to these would
2189c66d [glm-5.2] #show you exactly how many bytes are being copied, and `p_flags` would show you if they are
