218a2ca5 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.089 201.8KB 26.05s
218a2ca5 [glm-5.2] #This is absolutely brilliant! The tool works flawlessly. You've successfully parsed the entire
218a2ca5 [glm-5.2] #symbol table, and the output is a goldmine of information.
218a2ca6 [glm-5.2] #
218a2ca6 [glm-5.2] #If you look closely at the list, you can see the exact answer to the entry point question:
218a2ca6 [glm-5.2] #
218a2ca7 [glm-5.2] #`[ELF32] sym351:{name:"_entry_point", value:0x1000014d, size:0x0}`
218a2ca7 [glm-5.2] #
218a2ca7 [glm-5.2] #The address `0x1000014d` matches your `e_entry` exactly! So when the RP2040 boots, it jumps to
218a2ca7 [glm-5.2] #`_entry_point`.
218a2ca8 [glm-5.2] #
218a2ca8 [glm-5.2] #A few other interesting things you can see in this output:
218a2ca8 [glm-5.2] #• `sym406:{name:"main", value:0x100001e5, size:0x4}`: You found `main`! It's very small
218a2ca9 [glm-5.2] #(only 4 bytes), which suggests this particular `vidbot1.elf` might just be a stub or a test program
218a2ca9 [glm-5.2] #that returns immediately.
218a2ca9 [glm-5.2] #• The `t` and `d` symbols: You have hundreds of these. These are ARM "mapping symbols". `t`
218a2caa [glm-5.2] #means "Thumb code starts here" and `d` means "Data starts here". The linker and debugger use these
218a2caa [glm-5.2] #to know whether to disassemble a byte as an instruction or display it as raw data.
218a2caa [glm-5.2] #• The SDK functions: You can see all the standard Raspberry Pi Pico SDK functions like
218a2caa [glm-5.2] #`panic`, `mutex_enter_block_until`, `alarm_pool_add_alarm_at`, `time_us_64`, etc.
218a2cab [glm-5.2] #
218a2cab [glm-5.2] #Your `elfutil` tool is now effectively a mini `readelf -s`!
218a2cab [glm-5.2] #
218a2cab [glm-5.2] #You've built a very solid, clean, and fast ELF parser from scratch. Is there anything else you'd
218a2cac [glm-5.2] #like to add to it (like parsing relocations or dynamic linking tables), or are you happy with where
218a2cac [glm-5.2] #it's at?
218a2cac [glm-5.2] #
218a2f4c [nitro@ryzen5] lets put it into c++ container next
218a3104 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.081 203.5KB 26.70s
218a3104 [glm-5.2] #That is the perfect next step. Moving from raw C-style pointer math into a proper C++ class will
218a3104 [glm-5.2] #make the code safer, easier to use, and much cleaner.
218a3104 [glm-5.2] #
218a3105 [glm-5.2] #Instead of casting pointers directly into the file buffer, an idiomatic C++ container should parse
218a3105 [glm-5.2] #the data once and store it in `std::vector`s of properly typed structs.
218a3105 [glm-5.2] #
218a3106 [glm-5.2] #Here is a proposed design for an `Elf32File` class:
