218a605f [glm-5.2] #1. Potential Crash with `ptypes.find()`
218a605f [glm-5.2] #In your `main` loop, you have:
218a605f [glm-5.2] #
218a6060 [glm-5.2] #auto ptype=ptypes.find(ph[i].p_type)->second;
218a6060 [glm-5.2] #
218a6060 [glm-5.2] #If `p_type` is a value not in your map (like `0x6474e552`), `.find()` returns `ptypes.end()`.
218a6060 [glm-5.2] #Dereferencing `end()->second` will crash the program.
218a6061 [glm-5.2] #
218a6061 [glm-5.2] #A safer way to do it inline is:
218a6061 [glm-5.2] #
218a6062 [glm-5.2] #auto it = ptypes.find(ph[i].p_type);
218a6062 [glm-5.2] #std::string ptype = (it != ptypes.end()) ? it->second : "UNKNOWN";
218a6062 [glm-5.2] #
218a6062 [glm-5.2] #
218a6063 [glm-5.2] #2. Missing `inline` in `elf32.h`
218a6063 [glm-5.2] #You defined `pflags_string` directly in `elf32.h`:
218a6063 [glm-5.2] #
218a6063 [glm-5.2] #std::string pflags_string(uint32_t flags) { ... }
218a6064 [glm-5.2] #
218a6064 [glm-5.2] #Because it's a standard function (not a template or constexpr), if you ever include `elf32.h` in
218a6064 [glm-5.2] #more than one `.cpp` file, the linker will complain about "multiple definitions". You can fix this
218a6065 [glm-5.2] #by adding the `inline` keyword: `inline std::string pflags_string(...)`.
218a6065 [glm-5.2] #
218a6065 [glm-5.2] #3. Flags aren't printed yet
218a6065 [glm-5.2] #You added the `pflags_string` function, but you haven't actually called it in your `cout` statement
218a6066 [glm-5.2] #for the program headers! You can add it right after `filesz`:
