218c535a [glm-5.2] #A hex dump is a natural addition. A few things worth discussing before you start:
218c535a [glm-5.2] #
218c535a [glm-5.2] #• Offset model*
218c535a [glm-5.2] #
218c535a [glm-5.2] #Your `content` vector deliberately excludes the ELF header — every accessor subtracts
218c535b [glm-5.2] #`sizeof(elf32)`. That works for structured parsing but a hex dump should show true file offsets,
218c535b [glm-5.2] #otherwise users can't cross-reference with `objdump` or a hex editor. You have two options:
218c535b [glm-5.2] #
218c535b [glm-5.2] #- Dump directly from a re-read of the file (simplest, avoids the offset mismatch entirely).
218c535b [glm-5.2] #- Keep using `content` but add `sizeof(elf32)` back when printing offsets.
218c535b [glm-5.2] #
218c535c [glm-5.2] #The first is cleaner since a hex dump is inherently a raw view.
218c535c [glm-5.2] #
218c535c [glm-5.2] #• Scope*
218c535c [glm-5.2] #
218c535c [glm-5.2] #What granularity do you want?
218c535c [glm-5.2] #
218c535d [glm-5.2] #- Whole file
218c535d [glm-5.2] #- A section by name or index (using `sh_offset` / `sh_size`)
218c535d [glm-5.2] #- A segment by index (using `p_offset` / `p_filesz`)
218c535d [glm-5.2] #- An arbitrary byte range
218c535d [glm-5.2] #
218c535e [glm-5.2] #Section-scoped dumps are probably the most useful next step since you already enumerate sections
218c535e [glm-5.2] #and resolve their names. You could accept a section name as a second argument.
218c535e [glm-5.2] #
218c535e [glm-5.2] #• Format*
218c535e [glm-5.2] #
218c535f [glm-5.2] #The classic layout is:
218c535f [glm-5.2] #
218c535f [glm-5.2] #
218c535f [glm-5.2] #00000000  7f 45 4c 46 01 01 01 00  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  |.ELF............|
218c535f [glm-5.2] #
218c535f [glm-5.2] #
218c5360 [glm-5.2] #Offset in hex, 16 bytes per line with a gap at the midpoint, then ASCII with non-printables shown
218c5360 [glm-5.2] #as `.`. A column width of 16 is conventional but 8 can be useful for small sections.
218c5360 [glm-5.2] #
218c5360 [glm-5.2] #• One thing to watch*
218c5360 [glm-5.2] #
218c5361 [glm-5.2] #Your `etypes` vector is indexed by `e_type` directly:
218c5361 [glm-5.2] #
218c5361 [glm-5.2] #
218c5361 [glm-5.2] #std::cout << etypes[hdr.e_type]
218c5361 [glm-5.2] #
218c5362 [glm-5.2] #
218c5362 [glm-5.2] #If `e_type` is out of range that will crash. Not related to the hex dump but worth noting since
218c5362 [glm-5.2] #you'll be testing with varied files.
218c5362 [glm-5.2] #
218c5362 [glm-5.2] #Want to start with whole-file dump or section-scoped?
