219da211 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.009 18.46KB 5.28s
219da211 [glm-5.2] #Thank you for the update. I see the signature for `dumpMem` in `elf32.h` has been updated to:
219da211 [glm-5.2] #
219da211 [glm-5.2] #
219da211 [glm-5.2] #void dumpMem(const uint8_t* values, int base32, size_t size);
219da212 [glm-5.2] #
219da212 [glm-5.2] #
219da212 [glm-5.2] #This resolves the mismatch with the forward declaration and call site in `elfutil.cpp`. The rest of
219da212 [glm-5.2] #the header remains the same. Let me know if you plan to address the bounds checking on the
219da213 [glm-5.2] #`std::map::find` calls or if you need assistance with any other part of the utility.
219da213 [glm-5.2] #
219dab63 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.014 25.30KB 14.85s
219dab63 [glm-5.2] #Thank you for the updated file. I notice the attempt to add bounds checking in the program header
219dab63 [glm-5.2] #loop with:
219dab63 [glm-5.2] #
219dab63 [glm-5.2] #
219dab64 [glm-5.2] #auto ptype=ptypes.find(t)?->second;
219dab64 [glm-5.2] #
219dab64 [glm-5.2] #
219dab64 [glm-5.2] #The `?->` operator is not valid C++ syntax for `std::map::iterator`. While `std::optional` has
219dab65 [glm-5.2] #monadic operations proposed for C++23, standard iterators require an explicit check against
219dab65 [glm-5.2] #`end()`.
219dab65 [glm-5.2] #
219dab65 [glm-5.2] #Additionally, the section header loop still uses the unguarded
219dab66 [glm-5.2] #`shtypes.find(sh[i].sh_type)->second` pattern.
219dab66 [glm-5.2] #
219dab66 [glm-5.2] #To keep the code clean and avoid repeating the lookup logic, you might consider defining a named
219dab66 [glm-5.2] #reusable function for safe map lookups, returning a default string like `"UNKNOWN"` when the key is
219dab67 [glm-5.2] #missing. This aligns well with how `emachine_string` is already implemented.
219dab67 [glm-5.2] #
219dab67 [glm-5.2] #Let me know if you would like me to provide an example of such a function.
219dab67 [glm-5.2] #
219dbebc [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.016 35.30KB 12.12s
219dbebc [glm-5.2] #Thank you for the update. I see you've introduced a `typeString` function to handle the lookup
219dbebc [glm-5.2] #safely, which is a good step towards reusable code.
219dbebc [glm-5.2] #
219dbebd [glm-5.2] #However, there is a small issue in the implementation in `elf32.h`:
219dbebd [glm-5.2] #
219dbebd [glm-5.2] #
219dbebd [glm-5.2] #std::string typeString(uint32_t ptype){
219dbebe [glm-5.2] #	if(ptypes.has(ptype)) return ptypes.find(ptype)->second;
219dbebe [glm-5.2] #	return "[PTYPE_UNKNOWN]";
219dbebe [glm-5.2] #}
219dbebe [glm-5.2] #
219dbebf [glm-5.2] #
219dbebf [glm-5.2] #The `std::map` container does not have a `.has()` method in standard C++. You can use `.count()`
219dbebf [glm-5.2] #(which returns 1 if the key exists) or, if you are compiling with C++20 support, `.contains()`.
219dbec0 [glm-5.2] #
219dbec0 [glm-5.2] #Using `.count()` is the most broadly compatible approach:
219dbec0 [glm-5.2] #
219dbec0 [glm-5.2] #
219dbec1 [glm-5.2] #std::string typeString(uint32_t ptype) {
219dbec1 [glm-5.2] #	auto it = ptypes.find(ptype);
219dbec1 [glm-5.2] #	if (it != ptypes.end()) return it->second;
219dbec1 [glm-5.2] #	return "[PTYPE_UNKNOWN]";
219dbec2 [glm-5.2] #}
219dbec2 [glm-5.2] #
219dbec2 [glm-5.2] #
219dbec2 [glm-5.2] #Also, while the program header loop in `elfutil.cpp` now uses `typeString`, the section header loop
219dbec3 [glm-5.2] #still relies on the unguarded `shtypes.find(sh[i].sh_type)->second`. You might want to create a
219dbec3 [glm-5.2] #similar `shtypeString` function or generalize the lookup to cover both maps safely.
219dbec3 [glm-5.2] #
219dbec4 [glm-5.2] #Let me know if you would like me to provide an example of how to generalize the lookup function.
219dbec4 [glm-5.2] #
