219e064f [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.017 26.24KB 20.46s
219e064f [glm-5.2] #Thank you for sharing these files. I've reviewed the full set. Here are some observations:
219e064f [glm-5.2] #
219e0650 [glm-5.2] #• The codebase has clearly evolved since the relay.md transcript.* The suggestions from
219e0650 [glm-5.2] #that session (parse program headers, parse section headers, resolve section names via string table)
219e0650 [glm-5.2] #are all implemented in the current `elfutil.cpp`. The symbol table walk and entry-point symbol
219e0650 [glm-5.2] #lookup are also present, though `get_entry_symbol` is defined but not called in `main`.
219e0651 [glm-5.2] #
219e0651 [glm-5.2] #• One bug worth flagging* in `lmachine.h`, line in `llogic::isbranch()`:
219e0651 [glm-5.2] #
219e0651 [glm-5.2] #
219e0652 [glm-5.2] #inline int isbranch() {
219e0652 [glm-5.2] #    return (opcode&0x30==0x30);
219e0652 [glm-5.2] #}
219e0652 [glm-5.2] #
219e0653 [glm-5.2] #
219e0653 [glm-5.2] #Due to C++ operator precedence, `==` binds tighter than `&`, so this evaluates as `opcode & (0x30
219e0653 [glm-5.2] #== 0x30)` which is `opcode & 1`. It should be:
219e0654 [glm-5.2] #
219e0654 [glm-5.2] #
219e0654 [glm-5.2] #return ((opcode & 0x30) == 0x30);
219e0654 [glm-5.2] #
219e0655 [glm-5.2] #
219e0655 [glm-5.2] #• Other observations:*
219e0655 [glm-5.2] #
219e0655 [glm-5.2] #- The `content` vector deliberately excludes the 52-byte ELF header, and all subsequent offset
219e0656 [glm-5.2] #calculations subtract `sizeof(elf32)`. This is consistent throughout but fragile if someone adds a
219e0656 [glm-5.2] #new access path without accounting for it.
219e0656 [glm-5.2] #- There's no bounds checking when indexing into `content` via section or program header offsets. A
219e0656 [glm-5.2] #malformed ELF could cause out-of-bounds reads.
219e0657 [glm-5.2] #- `dumpThumb2` and `dumpMem` are declared but their implementations aren't in the shared files —
219e0657 [glm-5.2] #presumably they live in another translation unit.
219e0657 [glm-5.2] #- `thumb2.h` provides a comprehensive set of narrow and wide instruction encoders, but no
219e0658 [glm-5.2] #disassembler is visible. If `dumpThumb2` is meant to decode rather than just hex-dump, that
219e0658 [glm-5.2] #implementation would be interesting to see.
219e0658 [glm-5.2] #- The `rmachine`/`lmachine` hierarchy is an IR-to-native compilation framework with register
219e0658 [glm-5.2] #allocation and basic block analysis — `thumb2` as a code generator for RP2350/Cortex-M33 is a
219e0659 [glm-5.2] #concrete backend for it.
219e0659 [glm-5.2] #
219e0659 [glm-5.2] #Would you like to discuss any particular aspect further, or are you looking to extend the tool in a
219e0659 [glm-5.2] #specific direction?
219e065a [glm-5.2] #
