219ef9d6 [glm-5.2] #Thank you for sharing these files. I have reviewed the content and have a few observations:
219ef9d6 [glm-5.2] #
219ef9d6 [glm-5.2] #• Architecture overview*
219ef9d7 [glm-5.2] #
219ef9d7 [glm-5.2] #The codebase forms a small but coherent toolchain: `elfutil.cpp` loads and inspects ELF32 binaries,
219ef9d7 [glm-5.2] #`lmachine.h` provides an intermediate representation with basic-block register tracking,
219ef9d7 [glm-5.2] #`rmachine.h` is an abstract native-code backend, and `thumb2.h` / `thumb2dis.h` implement the
219ef9d7 [glm-5.2] #Cortex-M33 (RP2350) target as both a code generator and disassembler.
219ef9d8 [glm-5.2] #
219ef9d8 [glm-5.2] #• Observations*
219ef9d8 [glm-5.2] #
219ef9d9 [glm-5.2] #1. Offset convention — Throughout `elfutil.cpp`, raw offsets into the file are adjusted by
219ef9d9 [glm-5.2] #subtracting `sizeof(elf32)` because `content` excludes the header. This is internally consistent
219ef9d9 [glm-5.2] #but somewhat fragile; a future maintainer could easily miss the convention. A comment or a named
219ef9d9 [glm-5.2] #helper (e.g. `file_to_content_offset`) would help.
219ef9d9 [glm-5.2] #
219ef9da [glm-5.2] #2. `thumb2.h` operator precedence — Encoders like `mov_n` use `0x2000|d<<8|imm8`. This
219ef9da [glm-5.2] #works because `<<` binds tighter than `|`, but explicit parentheses would make the intent clearer
219ef9da [glm-5.2] #and avoid lint warnings.
219ef9da [glm-5.2] #
219ef9da [glm-5.2] #3. `thumb2dis.h` 32-bit detection — The condition `(code & 0xF800) >= 0xE800` correctly
219ef9db [glm-5.2] #captures all three 32-bit Thumb prefixes (0xE800–0xFFFF). Good.
219ef9db [glm-5.2] #
219ef9db [glm-5.2] #4. `thumb2dis.h` indentation — `disasm_32bit` and `dumpThumb2` are at file scope but the
219ef9db [glm-5.2] #indentation drifts inward partway through, suggesting a paste or merge artifact. The logic is fine;
219ef9dc [glm-5.2] #only formatting is affected.
219ef9dc [glm-5.2] #
219ef9dc [glm-5.2] #5. Unused functions — `get_entry_symbol` is defined but never called, and `print_symbols`
219ef9dc [glm-5.2] #is commented out in `main`. Presumably these are toggled during development.
219ef9dd [glm-5.2] #
219ef9dd [glm-5.2] #6. `thumb2dis.h` coverage — The 32-bit disassembler handles BL, B.W, Bcc.W, MOVW, and MOVT,
219ef9dd [glm-5.2] #falling back to `DCD` for everything else. If you plan to extend it, the next high-value additions
219ef9de [glm-5.2] #would be the wide LDR/STR forms and the ADD/SUB wide immediate encodings, since those appear
219ef9de [glm-5.2] #frequently in compiler-generated RP2350 code.
219ef9de [glm-5.2] #
219ef9de [glm-5.2] #7. `lmachine.h` — The register lifetime tracking in `lblock` (distinguishing `ilist` vs
219ef9df [glm-5.2] #`ulist`) is a neat approach for a simple register allocator. The `weldreg`/`weldable` interface
219ef9df [glm-5.2] #suggests copy-coalescing support, which is a nice touch for a compact backend.
219ef9df [glm-5.2] #
219ef9df [glm-5.2] #Nothing appears broken or incorrect in what is present. Let me know if you would like me to help
219ef9e0 [glm-5.2] #extend the disassembler coverage, clean up formatting, or assist with any other part of the
219ef9e0 [glm-5.2] #toolchain.
219ef9e0 [glm-5.2] #
