219fff73 [glm-5.2] #Thank you for sharing these files. Here are some observations on the content:
219fff74 [glm-5.2] #
219fff74 [glm-5.2] #• elfutil.cpp / elf32.h*
219fff74 [glm-5.2] #
219fff74 [glm-5.2] #The ELF32 parser is taking shape nicely. A few things I noticed:
219fff74 [glm-5.2] #
219fff75 [glm-5.2] #1. Offset convention — The `content` vector excludes the ELF header (it starts after
219fff75 [glm-5.2] #`sizeof(elf32)`), so all offset calculations subtract `sizeof(elf32)`. This works but is a subtle
219fff75 [glm-5.2] #convention that could be fragile. An alternative would be to load the entire file into `content`
219fff75 [glm-5.2] #and index from zero, which would let you use raw `sh_offset`/`p_offset` values directly.
219fff76 [glm-5.2] #
219fff76 [glm-5.2] #2. `get_shstrtab` bounds check — There is a guard for `e_shstrndx >= e_shnum`, but no
219fff76 [glm-5.2] #validation that `strsec.sh_offset` actually lands within `content`. A malformed ELF could cause an
219fff77 [glm-5.2] #out-of-bounds read.
219fff77 [glm-5.2] #
219fff77 [glm-5.2] #3. `shtypes.find()` in the section loop — If `sh[i].sh_type` is not in the map,
219fff77 [glm-5.2] #`find()->second` dereferences `end()` and is undefined behavior. The program header loop uses
219fff78 [glm-5.2] #`typeString()` which handles this safely; a similar helper for section types would be prudent.
219fff78 [glm-5.2] #
219fff78 [glm-5.2] #4. `print_symbols` is commented out in `main` but `get_entry_symbol` is defined and never
219fff78 [glm-5.2] #called. Presumably these are queued for upcoming use.
219fff79 [glm-5.2] #
219fff79 [glm-5.2] #5. Alignment — Casting `content.data()` to `elf32_sym` / `elf32_phdr` /
219fff79 [glm-5.2] #`elf32_shdr*` assumes the `std::vector<char>` buffer satisfies the alignment requirements of those
219fff79 [glm-5.2] #structs. On x86 this typically works; on stricter architectures it could fault.
219fff7a [glm-5.2] #
219fff7a [glm-5.2] #• bot1.cpp*
219fff7a [glm-5.2] #
219fff7b [glm-5.2] #`main` returns `1`, which conventionally signals an error to the host environment. If this is a
219fff7b [glm-5.2] #stub placeholder that is fine, but `return 0` would be the norm for a successful no-op.
219fff7b [glm-5.2] #
219fff7b [glm-5.2] #• welcome.txt*
219fff7b [glm-5.2] #
