21a02d12 [glm-5.2] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.013 14.58KB 39.21s
21a02d12 [glm-5.2] #When considering a basic CRC for testing a disassembler, you have a few standard options. The
21a02d13 [glm-5.2] #choice between them dictates what kinds of instructions the compiler will emit, so it is worth
21a02d13 [glm-5.2] #picking one that covers the architectural behaviors you want to verify.
21a02d13 [glm-5.2] #
21a02d13 [glm-5.2] #• 1. CRC-32 (IEEE 802.3)*
21a02d14 [glm-5.2] #This is the most common 32-bit checksum, used in Ethernet and ZIP files.
21a02d14 [glm-5.2] #• Disassembler Coverage: Because it operates on 32-bit words, it heavily exercises bitwise
21a02d14 [glm-5.2] #shifts (`LSR`), exclusive ORs (`EOR`), and bit testing (`TST` or `AND`). Processing an input buffer
21a02d14 [glm-5.2] #will generate `LDRB` (Load Register Byte) instructions and pointer arithmetic.
21a02d15 [glm-5.2] #• Thumb-2 Specifics: The inner loop typically involves shifting a 32-bit register right by
21a02d15 [glm-5.2] #1, testing the lowest bit, and conditionally XORing with a polynomial (like `0xEDB88320`). This
21a02d15 [glm-5.2] #generates excellent conditional branch (`BNE`/`BEQ`) sequences.
21a02d15 [glm-5.2] #
21a02d16 [glm-5.2] #• 2. CRC-16 (CCITT)*
21a02d16 [glm-5.2] #This is a 16-bit variant often used in serial communications and embedded systems.
21a02d16 [glm-5.2] #• Disassembler Coverage: It generates a similar instruction profile to CRC-32 but might
21a02d16 [glm-5.2] #force the compiler to use 16-bit specific instructions or explicit zero-extension instructions
21a02d17 [glm-5.2] #(like `UXTH` - Zero Extend Halfword) to keep the upper 16 bits of the 32-bit registers clear. This
21a02d17 [glm-5.2] #is highly useful for testing those specific Thumb-2 extension instructions.
21a02d17 [glm-5.2] #
21a02d17 [glm-5.2] #• 3. Table-Driven CRC vs. Bitwise CRC*
21a02d18 [glm-5.2] #How you implement the CRC mathematically changes the output binary significantly:
21a02d18 [glm-5.2] #• Bitwise: Calculates the CRC bit-by-bit. This generates tight, predictable loops with
21a02d18 [glm-5.2] #logical operations and conditional branches. It is the best choice for testing core ALU operations
21a02d18 [glm-5.2] #and branch offset decoding.
21a02d19 [glm-5.2] #• Table-Driven: Uses a precomputed 256-entry array to process one byte at a time. This
21a02d19 [glm-5.2] #shifts the focus from bitwise logic to memory operations. It will generate `LDR` instructions with
21a02d19 [glm-5.2] #computed shifted offsets (e.g., shifting an index left by 2 bytes and adding it to a base address),
21a02d19 [glm-5.2] #which tests complex addressing modes but hides the logical bitwise operations behind a memory
21a02d1a [glm-5.2] #lookup.
21a02d1a [glm-5.2] #
21a02d1a [glm-5.2] #• Recommendation for Testing*
21a02d1a [glm-5.2] #A bitwise CRC-32 function that takes a `uint8_t*` data pointer and a `size_t` length is an
21a02d1b [glm-5.2] #excellent target. It will yield a public symbol, a prologue/epilogue, stack frame management,
21a02d1b [glm-5.2] #pointer iteration, byte loads, and a dense inner loop of bitwise logic and branching.
21a02d1b [glm-5.2] #
