21e7a5b3 [grok-build-0.1] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.010 35.94KB 12.46s
21e7a5b3 [grok-build-0.1] #• Yes, happy to help with ASCII maze tips.*
21e7a5b3 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b3 [grok-build-0.1] #Here are some focused, practical tips based on the boxChars and strips you’ve already collected:
21e7a5b4 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b4 [grok-build-0.1] #1. Keep a Consistent Wall Set
21e7a5b4 [grok-build-0.1] #Pick one boxChars set for an entire maze and stick to it. Mixing light, double, and heavy in the
21e7a5b4 [grok-build-0.1] #same layout usually looks messy in text.
21e7a5b5 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b5 [grok-build-0.1] #- Rounded (`╭╮╰╯─┬┴│┤├┼`) works well for organic or softer mazes.
21e7a5b5 [grok-build-0.1] #- Light (`┌┐└┘─┬┴│┤├┼`) is the safest for readability at small sizes.
21e7a5b5 [grok-build-0.1] #- Double (`╔╗╚╝═╦╩║╣╠╬`) gives strong contrast and works great for outer boundaries.
21e7a5b6 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b6 [grok-build-0.1] #• Tip:* If you want to show different wall strengths (e.g. normal walls vs. thick outer
21e7a5b6 [grok-build-0.1] #walls), use two different sets side-by-side rather than mixing inside the same grid.
21e7a5b6 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b7 [grok-build-0.1] #2. Decide How to Handle “Strips”
21e7a5b7 [grok-build-0.1] #Your mathematical strips (`⊏⊐⊑⊒⋢⋣⊂⊃⊆⊇⊈⊉`) are useful but break the pure wall-drawing logic. Treat
21e7a5b7 [grok-build-0.1] #them as special cell types rather than walls.
21e7a5b7 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b8 [grok-build-0.1] #Good approaches:
21e7a5b8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Replace a full cell with a strip when it represents a gate/door/one-way.
21e7a5b8 [grok-build-0.1] #- Or draw the strip inside a 2×2 or 3×3 block so the surrounding box characters can still connect
21e7a5b8 [grok-build-0.1] #properly.
21e7a5b9 [grok-build-0.1] #- Keep the strips on their own layer so you can toggle them on/off without redrawing walls.
21e7a5b9 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5b9 [grok-build-0.1] #3. Use a Bitmask (or similar) for Reliable Rendering
21e7a5ba [grok-build-0.1] #Instead of hand-drawing every corner, store each cell as a 4-bit value (North/East/South/West
21e7a5ba [grok-build-0.1] #connections). This makes generation and rendering much more consistent.
21e7a5ba [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5ba [grok-build-0.1] #Common quick checks:
21e7a5bb [grok-build-0.1] #- Calculate the correct junction character from the four directions.
21e7a5bb [grok-build-0.1] #- Always draw the outer perimeter first, then internal walls.
21e7a5bb [grok-build-0.1] #- Leave at least one space between walls if you want room for the player or objects.
21e7a5bb [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5bc [grok-build-0.1] #4. Terminal & Font Realities
21e7a5bc [grok-build-0.1] #- Always test in a true monospace font (Consolas, Courier New, JetBrains Mono, etc.).
21e7a5bc [grok-build-0.1] #- Some terminals clip the top or bottom of box-drawing characters. Rounded and light sets are
21e7a5bc [grok-build-0.1] #usually more forgiving than heavy or double.
21e7a5bd [grok-build-0.1] #- UTF-8 output is required. If characters show as `?` or boxes, force UTF-8 encoding.
21e7a5bd [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5bd [grok-build-0.1] #5. Visual Clarity Tips
21e7a5bd [grok-build-0.1] #- Long straight runs look better with the continuous line characters (`─` `│` `═` `║`) than
21e7a5be [grok-build-0.1] #repeated corners.
21e7a5be [grok-build-0.1] #- Avoid too many `┼` crosses in small mazes — they can make the layout feel noisy.
21e7a5be [grok-build-0.1] #- For dead-ends, the single-line terminators (╵ ╶ ╷ ╴ etc.) are cleaner than forcing a corner
21e7a5be [grok-build-0.1] #character.
21e7a5bf [grok-build-0.1] #- When using strips, keep them visually distinct from walls so players immediately understand
21e7a5bf [grok-build-0.1] #they’re special.
21e7a5bf [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5bf [grok-build-0.1] #6. Quick Recognition in Your Own ASCII
21e7a5c0 [grok-build-0.1] #If you want to quickly tell what kind of maze you’ve generated:
21e7a5c0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Lots of short stubs → looks like Prim/Kruskal.
21e7a5c0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Long winding corridors with few branches → recursive backtracker.
21e7a5c0 [grok-build-0.1] #- Strong top and right edges open → binary tree or sidewinder.
21e7a5c1 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5c1 [grok-build-0.1] #Would you like tips focused on any specific area? For example:
21e7a5c1 [grok-build-0.1] #- How to cleanly combine your strips with the box characters
21e7a5c1 [grok-build-0.1] #- Simple rules for rule-based generation that look good in ASCII
21e7a5c2 [grok-build-0.1] #- How to represent the famous real-life designs (Cretan, Chartres, etc.) in text
21e7a5c2 [grok-build-0.1] #- Or debugging tricks when the walls don’t connect properly
21e7a5c2 [grok-build-0.1] #
21e7a5c2 [grok-build-0.1] #Just let me know which direction to go.
21e7a5c3 [grok-build-0.1] #
