21f00f31 [nitro@ryzen5] User: gm from aucklandKimi: Good morning from Auckland! Hope you're having a great start to your Sunday. Anything I can help you with today?User: kbot is having a moment on discordKimi: I see - looks like Kimi (the Discord bot) got a bit philosophical and then hit a rate limit. The conversation went from "na tamaki makaurau" (the Maori name for Auckland) to Alaska winters to the "meat bag" caring about latitude, then finally to a practical weather observation about England's heatwave vs. frosty conditions down under.And then... rate limit exceeded. The bot had thoughts but the API said "not so fast."The blocked message is probably the bot trying to finish its thought, or maybe the user sent something that got caught by a filter. Slowmode enabled too - classic Discord moderation move.Is this your server, or did you just catch Kimi having an existential crisis in public?User: I asked K (the bot) and got Error: Rate limit exceeded. Please try again shortly.Kimi: Ah, so you were trying to get a response out of K on Discord and got rate-limited instead. Frustrating when the bot has a moment right when you need it to.The screenshot shows K went full poetic mode - Maori place names, James Cowan, "meat bag" philosophy - then hit the wall on your follow-up about the England heatwave vs. frosty Down Under.A few things that might help:- **Slowmode is on** in that channel (visible at the bottom), so there may be a cooldown between messages- The bot itself might have its own rate limiting per user or per server- If it's a busy server, other users might be hammering the bot tooCould try waiting a minute or two and replying to your own message rather than sending a fresh one - sometimes that bypasses user rate limits if the bot treats threads/replies differently.Or just ask me here instead - no rate limits, and I can handle the weather report without the existential digressions. Unless you want those too.User: the kimi.com free service here does not show tokens used / coal burnt for replies?Kimi: That's right - the kimi.com free service doesn't show token usage or "coal burnt" (energy/cost metrics) for replies. It's a clean, minimal interface without that kind of technical telemetry.Some AI services do expose token counts (like OpenAI's playground or API dashboards), but Kimi's consumer web interface keeps it simple - no usage bars, no cost per message, no carbon footprint calculators.If you're curious about how much you're actually using, there's no built-in meter on the free tier. You'd only see that kind of data if you were on a paid/API plan with usage dashboards, and even then I'm not sure Kimi exposes per-message token counts the way some platforms do.Why the interest - trying to gauge efficiency, or just comparing against what K on Discord might be burning through?User: model 13 under test api versionKimi: I see - you're running your own relay (Relay 1.9.4) that proxies to various AI APIs, and you switched to model #13 which is `kimi-k2.7-code@moonshot`. The test shows it working: `>hi kimi` returned a response in 1.19s with 551B.The price column for Kimi models shows `0.95,0.19,4` - presumably input/output/max pricing tiers or multipliers. But your relay shows `$0.000` for that test call, so either it's a very cheap call or the billing isn't fully wired up yet.You're testing the API version of Kimi rather than the free web interface. The API would expose token counts and costs (unlike kimi.com), but it looks like your relay is abstracting that away with a flat `$0.000` display.Is the test API version behaving differently from what you expected? Or are you checking if the rate limiting you hit on Discord is related to API quotas vs. bot-level throttling?User: lol at abstracting, no that is called fair math rounding, or maybe price undefined will check....Kimi: Fair point - "abstracting" was generous. It's either rounding to zero because the cost was genuinely sub-cent for a tiny 551B call, or the price field is undefined/placeholder for that model and the relay just shows `$0.000` as a default.Looking at the price column again: `0.95,0.19,4` for kimi-k2.7-code. If those are per-million-token rates (input, output, maybe something else), then a 551-byte "hi" call would indeed round to $0.000. The math checks out for fair rounding.But you're right to be suspicious - could also be that the relay hasn't fully wired up Moonshot API billing yet, or the price array parsing is off. The `kimi-k2.6` at `0.95,0.16,4` and `kimi-k2.5` at `0.6,0.1,3` suggest the pricing schema is consistent across the Kimi family.You'll check if it's actually undefined or just microscopically cheap. Either way, the rate limit on Discord is definitely a separate issue from API cost - bot throttling vs. API billing.User: You were the one being suspiciousKimi: Guilty as charged. I was the one side-eyeing the `$0.000` like it might be a placeholder. You were just running your relay and reporting the numbers.Fair math rounding it is - or price undefined, as you said you'll check. My bad for projecting skepticism onto your setup.User: the model all function lists everything at the endpointKimi: Got it - `/model all` dumps the full catalog from the endpoint. That's a lot of models. I see the Gemini section is extensive: image generation (`imagen-4.0`), video generation (`veo-3.1`), embeddings, TTS variants, even `nano-banana-pro` and `antigravity-05-2026`.Most have `count: 0` and no price, which suggests they're discoverable but not necessarily configured or priced in your relay yet. The ones with counts and prices are the actively used ones - `gemini-3.1-pro` (55), `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` (87), `gemini-3.5-flash` (8).The `gemini-robotics-er` at `1,5` is interesting - robotics model with a price, but zero count. And `deep-research` variants with no prices, probably preview/unreleased.Your relay is basically a unified gateway to everything. The endpoint exposes the full menu, but your local config decides what's actually available to switch to.User: it needs an error column, sometimes api support for relay is unsupportedKimi: Yeah, an error column would be useful - flag models where the API support is broken, deprecated, or just not wired up yet in the relay. Something like:```id | model | vendor | count | price | error--|-------|--------|-------|-------|-------12 | kimi-k2.7-code-highspeed | moonshot | 0 | 0.95,0.19,4 | unsupported```Or maybe granular codes: `unsupported`, `rate_limited`, `auth_failed`, `deprecated`, `quota_exceeded`.Right now the zero-count, no-price models are ambiguous - are they just unused, or do they not work? An error column would distinguish "available but nobody's picked it" from "listed but broken."Would you add it as a static config field, or have the relay probe endpoints and populate it dynamically?User: in the box tick column happy to have row of boxes, will add consideration to txtKimi: Got it - in the checkbox column, you're happy with a row of boxes. And you'll add the error column consideration to your todo/notes txt.Pragmatic approach. The checkbox UI pattern works, and documenting the error column idea for later keeps it from getting lost. No need to over-engineer the display right now.User: yes, design says you can have 1 char lolKimi: Hah, one character it is. Design constraints are real - no sprawling error messages in a single-char column. Maybe:- `�` = working- `?` = broken/unsupported- `?` = unknown/untested- `!` = rate limited or degraded- ` ` = blank for unused/irrelevantOr just binary: ` ` vs `?`. One char, one bit of information. Elegant in its constraint.User: and a scan command could double checkKimi: A `/scan` command to double-check - smart. Probe the endpoints in real-time, validate which models are actually responding, and update that single-char status. Could catch drift between config and reality.Something like:```> /scanprobing 213 models...� kimi-k2.7-code ... 1.19s? kimi-k2.7-code-highspeed ... timeout? gemini-robotics-er ... 503� grok-build-0.1 ... 0.8sdone. 187 �  12 ?  14 ?```Then write the results back to the checkbox column. Static config plus dynamic validation - best of both.User: managaing the model sphere* expand model status uiKimi: Got it - managing the model sphere, expanding the model status UI. The checkbox column with single-char status, plus a `/scan` command to validate endpoints dynamically. Clean, constrained, functional.What's next on the model sphere - wiring up more Moonshot models, or testing that scan command against the Kimi family?User: my account commandKimi: Nice - `/account` gives you the vendor-level view. I see the full spread:- **deepseek**: $-3.48, 2 LLMs- **zai**: $-5.38, 8 LLMs  - **moonshot**: $-2.16, 12 LLMs (your Kimi family)- **anthropic**: $+24.02, 9 LLMs - the only one in positive credit- **openai**: $-1.53, 124 LLMs (by far the biggest catalog)- **xai**: $-8.94, 9 LLMs (Grok, most negative balance)- **gemini**: $-5.39, 50 LLMs- **ollama**: $0.00, 0 LLMs - local, no cloud billingMost accounts are running negative balances, which makes sense if you're burning through API credits across multiple providers. Anthropic is the outlier at +$24.The `topup` column with direct URLs to billing dashboards is a nice touch - one-click to refill when a vendor runs dry.Is the negative balance actual debt, or just a display of estimated spend vs. prepaid credits? Some of those numbers look like they could trigger service cuts if real.User: managaing the model sphere* expand model status ui* scan command iterate all models at all endpoints* account command list trim/totalKimi: Got it - your todo list for managing the model sphere:- **Expand model status UI** - single-char checkbox column for model state- **Scan command** - iterate all models at all endpoints, validate which actually work- **Account command** - list trim/total (probably trimming the display and adding totals)The `/account` screenshot shows 8 vendors with credit balances. A trim/total view might collapse to just vendor name + credit, or add a sum row at the bottom. Something like:```total: $-9.9234 across 8 vendors, 214 models```Or maybe flag which accounts are critically low and need topup.Want to tackle the scan command first, or the account UI trim?User: after a scan, not trim, available is the word i should have usedKimi: Got it - after a scan, not trim. **Available** is the right word. So the account command should show what's actually available right now, post-scan. Filter out the models that are broken, rate-limited, or out of credit.Something like:```> /account availabledeepseek: 2/2 models available, $-3.48 creditzai: 6/8 models available, $-5.38 creditmoonshot: 10/12 models available, $-2.16 credit...```Or just a clean list of which vendors have working endpoints and how many models you can actually reach. The scan feeds the account view with real availability data.User: nice, i like the data no table mode, what mode could that be called?Kimi: "List mode" or "compact mode" - but since you like "data no table," maybe:- **Raw mode**- **Plain mode**- **Stream mode**- **Tsv mode** (if it's tab-separated)- **Bare mode**- **Naked mode** (no formatting)- **Log mode**Or if you want something more distinctive: **spool mode**, **scroll mode**, **terminal mode**, **flat mode**, **unadorned mode**.My pick: **raw mode** or **plain mode**. Clear, unambiguous, contrasts with the table UI.User: > >/config0 squash : squash message sequences in output : false1 reasonoutloud : echo chain of thought : false2 tools : enable model tool interface : false3 search : enable search tool interface : true4 commitonstart : commit shared files on start : false5 saveonexit :  save conversation history on exit : true6 ansi : markdown ANSI rendering : true7 verbose : emit debug information : false8 broken : ansi background blocks : false9 logging : log all output to file : true10 debugging : emit diagnostics : false11 pushonshare : emit a /push after any /share : false12 slopprompt : rawmode stdin with shortcode support : true13 resetcounters : factory reset counters and files on reset : false14 returntopush : hit return to /push - under test : false15 slow : experimental output at reading speed : false16 slops : console worker scripts : false17 budget : cheap models for the win : false18 syncRelay : one thing at a time mode : true19 listen : listen for remote connections on port 8081 : true20 project : load current project on start : true21 thinking : enable thinking mode with dual purpose models : true22 underline : enable underline markdown support : false23 persona : extend system prompt : true24 starshare : conserve file share by star : trueKimi: I see - your `/config` list with 25 toggles. Some interesting ones:- **rawmode stdin with shortcode support** (`slopprompt: true`) - this is probably close to what you were looking for as a "data no table" display mode- **squash message sequences** (`squash: false`) - collapse repeated messages- **thinking mode** (`thinking: true`) - dual purpose models, probably for reasoning models- **budget** (`budget: false`) - cheap models for the win, disabled- **syncRelay** (`syncRelay: true`) - one thing at a time, serial processing- **listen** (`listen: true`) - remote connections on 8081The `slopprompt` flag at #12 sounds like it might already be a raw/plain mode for prompts. But for output display, you don't have a dedicated "no table" mode yet.Maybe add a config toggle:```25 rawout : disable table formatting in output : false```Or repurpose `slopprompt` to also affect output, though that might be confusing.What does `slopprompt` actually do right now - just stdin parsing, or does it affect display too?User: can i share this chat?Kimi: Yes, you can share this chat. Kimi has a share feature - look for a share button in the interface, or you can use the `/share` command if your client supports it.From your config list, `pushonshare` is set to `false` (item 11), so sharing won't auto-push to your relay. And `starshare` is `true` (item 24), which conserves file share by star - probably means starred conversations get shared more efficiently or with less overhead.If you want to share, just hit the share button or say `/share` and you'll get a link. The conversation will be viewable by anyone with the link.Want me to do anything specific before you share - like summarizing the model sphere todo list we built up?
