22212be8 [deepseek-v4-flash] # ꔀ Relay 1.9.4 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.009 102.2KB 8.14s
22212be8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Yes, `Uint8Array` is commonly used to access WebAssembly linear memory from JavaScript.
22212be8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
22212be8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- WebAssembly linear memory is a contiguous block of bytes, exposed as a `WebAssembly.Memory`
22212be9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #object.
22212be9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- That memory object has a `.buffer` property which is an `ArrayBuffer` (or `SharedArrayBuffer` for
22212be9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #shared memory between threads).
22212be9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- You create a `Uint8Array` view over that buffer to read and write bytes directly.
22212bea [deepseek-v4-flash] #
22212bea [deepseek-v4-flash] #For example:
22212bea [deepseek-v4-flash] #
22212bea [deepseek-v4-flash] #const memory = new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: 1 });  // 64 KB
22212beb [deepseek-v4-flash] #const bytes = new Uint8Array(memory.buffer);
22212beb [deepseek-v4-flash] #bytes[0] = 42;   // write to Wasm memory
22212beb [deepseek-v4-flash] #
22212bec [deepseek-v4-flash] #
22212bec [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you need shared memory (e.g., between a main thread and a worker, or between multiple
22212bec [deepseek-v4-flash] #Wasm instances), you use `SharedArrayBuffer` and pass it to `WebAssembly.Memory` with `shared:
22212bec [deepseek-v4-flash] #true`. Then you can create `Uint8Array` views on that shared buffer from multiple contexts – but
22212bed [deepseek-v4-flash] #you must use `Atomics` for safe concurrent access.
22212bed [deepseek-v4-flash] #
22212bed [deepseek-v4-flash] #So yes, `Uint8Array` is the standard tool for sharing byte‑level memory between JavaScript and
22212bed [deepseek-v4-flash] #WebAssembly.
22212bee [deepseek-v4-flash] #
