2221dcd2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2221dcd2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2221dcd3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #You treat each source byte as four 2‑bit values (two bit pairs per byte row). The quad character
2221dcd3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #then maps the upper 2‑bit value from the row `y2` to the low nibble of the index, and
2221dcd3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the lower 2‑bit value from the next row (`y2+1`) to the high nibble. That gives a 4‑bit
2221dcd3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #index where the two bits from each row represent two horizontally adjacent pixels (or a 2‑level
2221dcd4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #intensity).
2221dcd4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2221dcd4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #This is a valid design – it allows a 2‑bit‑per‑pixel grayscale (4 levels) encoded in the `quads`
2221dcd4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #array. The `quads` array you provided (`" ▘▝▀▖▌▞▛▗▚▐▜▄▙▟█"`) actually matches the 2×2 quadrant
2221dcd5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #pattern, but you're using it to represent 2‑bit combinations (not 1‑bit quadrants). That's clever –
2221dcd5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #each quad character here corresponds to a 2‑bit row pair, not the classic four‑corner bits.
2221dcd5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
2221dcd5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So your code is consistent with that approach, and my earlier "fix" would have broken that design.
2221dcd6 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Thank you for correcting me.
