222f5760 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #In Deno (and Node.js), `Deno.stdout.write()` is already synchronous and unbuffered when
222f5761 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #writing to a terminal or a pipe. There is no explicit `flush()` method needed because the data is
222f5761 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #sent to the OS immediately.
222f5761 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #
222f5761 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #However, there is a common pitfall: Buffering at the Terminal Emulator level.
222f5762 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #
222f5762 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #Why it might seem like it needs a flush:
222f5762 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #If you are writing many small sequences (like mouse codes and cursor movements) in a very tight
222f5762 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #loop, the terminal emulator (the window you are looking at) might be delaying the visual update to
222f5763 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #save CPU.
222f5763 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #
222f5763 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #If you feel it's lagging:
222f5763 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #1.  Use `Deno.stdout.sync()`: If you want to ensure the OS has physically handed the bytes
222f5764 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #to the terminal, you can call `Deno.stdout.sync()`. This forces a flush of any kernel-level
222f5764 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #buffers.
222f5764 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #
222f5764 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #    Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(mouseOn));
222f5765 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #    Deno.stdout.sync();
222f5765 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #
222f5765 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #2.  Use `writeSync` instead of `write`: Since `write` returns a Promise (making it
222f5765 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #asynchronous), it can occasionally cause interleaving if you have multiple parts of your code
222f5766 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #trying to write to the terminal simultaneously. In a terminal UI loop, `Deno.stdout.writeSync` is
222f5766 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #often more reliable and prevents "tearing" or lag.
222f5766 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] #
